1
00:00:10,909 --> 00:00:12,640
(A Reassuring Start)

2
00:00:12,740 --> 00:00:13,980
It's good to see you!

3
00:00:13,980 --> 00:00:15,750
- Good to see you. - Hello.

4
00:00:16,009 --> 00:00:19,009
The Year of the Blue Snake, 2025 has dawned.

5
00:00:19,009 --> 00:00:20,679
- Happy New Year. - Happy New Year.

6
00:00:20,679 --> 00:00:21,779
Happy New Year.

7
00:00:21,779 --> 00:00:24,289
To welcome the New Year, I prepared a gift for the members...

8
00:00:24,390 --> 00:00:25,890
as someone who was born...

9
00:00:26,359 --> 00:00:27,989
- in the Year of the Snake. - It's a New Year's money gift!

10
00:00:28,960 --> 00:00:31,230
- You didn't have to prepare them. - Do a deep bow before saying that!

11
00:00:31,230 --> 00:00:33,429
- Let's give him a deep bow. - Don't.

12
00:00:33,559 --> 00:00:35,199
- No. - It's about time we did one.

13
00:00:35,359 --> 00:00:38,230
- I can only wire you. Sit down. - Really? That's good enough for me.

14
00:00:38,230 --> 00:00:40,070
- Give me your watch, at least. - I don't have one.

15
00:00:40,070 --> 00:00:42,200
- "Give me your watch, at least." - He's not wearing one today.

16
00:00:42,640 --> 00:00:44,310
Ta-da.

17
00:00:44,670 --> 00:00:48,439
I brought this so I could feed you something delicious.

18
00:00:48,780 --> 00:00:50,409
- Ta-da. - Goodness.

19
00:00:50,509 --> 00:00:52,450
- What is it? Is it rice cake? - Yes, it's rice cake.

20
00:00:52,520 --> 00:00:54,820
I was hoping we'd have a good start after sharing rice cake.

21
00:00:54,950 --> 00:00:57,020
- That's why I got rice cake. - Goodness.

22
00:00:57,250 --> 00:01:00,390
- All right. Line up. - Goodness.

23
00:01:00,390 --> 00:01:01,890
- Here you go. - It's really fascinating.

24
00:01:02,090 --> 00:01:04,630
Gosh, the scent is amazing.

25
00:01:04,630 --> 00:01:05,829
The scent is crazy good.

26
00:01:06,100 --> 00:01:07,960
Thank you for this, Hyun Moo.

27
00:01:07,960 --> 00:01:10,229
- It's really soft. Right? - Yes.

28
00:01:10,729 --> 00:01:11,899
- It's good. - It's good.

29
00:01:11,899 --> 00:01:13,099
It has a rich taste of black sesame, right?

30
00:01:13,370 --> 00:01:14,769
It's really delicious.

31
00:01:14,769 --> 00:01:16,840
(A filling beginning of the New Year)

32
00:01:16,840 --> 00:01:18,939
Let's start off in a good light.

33
00:01:18,939 --> 00:01:21,980
Let's check out KEY's day, which he spent meaningfully.

34
00:01:22,780 --> 00:01:24,379
(A Shining Graduation Certificate)

35
00:01:24,379 --> 00:01:25,510
A Shining Graduation Certificate?

36
00:01:26,680 --> 00:01:28,480
(Clothes scattered on the floor)

37
00:01:29,349 --> 00:01:31,450
(Left open)

38
00:01:33,459 --> 00:01:37,329
(Squirming under a flower blanket)

39
00:01:39,200 --> 00:01:41,430
- Wait. This isn't his house. - A flower blanket?

40
00:01:41,430 --> 00:01:43,099
- What is this? - Where is that?

41
00:01:43,099 --> 00:01:44,170
Where is he?

42
00:01:44,900 --> 00:01:46,140
It's my parent's place.

43
00:01:46,200 --> 00:01:47,569
- Oh, your parents' place? - In Daegu?

44
00:01:47,569 --> 00:01:48,670
Yes, my parents' place in Daegu.

45
00:01:48,670 --> 00:01:52,939
I slept over at my parents' place in Daegu after a long time.

46
00:01:54,409 --> 00:01:55,780
Your mom must've loved it.

47
00:01:55,780 --> 00:01:57,049
- Goodness. - She did.

48
00:01:57,750 --> 00:02:00,620
- That's right. - Today is...

49
00:02:00,620 --> 00:02:02,590
a really big day for our family.

50
00:02:02,790 --> 00:02:06,760
It's the last day of work for my mother, Ms. Kim Sun Hee.

51
00:02:06,760 --> 00:02:08,219
(The last day of work)

52
00:02:08,760 --> 00:02:10,329
(KEY prepared a trip for his mother last time.)

53
00:02:10,329 --> 00:02:13,230
- Oh, right. They went to Japan. - That's right.

54
00:02:14,030 --> 00:02:16,469
(A gift his mother loved)

55
00:02:17,169 --> 00:02:19,340
(A sweet mother-son trip that made everyone envious)

56
00:02:20,000 --> 00:02:21,740
(She worked as a head nurse in 2022.)

57
00:02:22,439 --> 00:02:24,210
(Then she got promoted to a chief.)

58
00:02:24,210 --> 00:02:25,539
That's right! You celebrated her promotion...

59
00:02:25,539 --> 00:02:27,439
- in Japan. - That's right.

60
00:02:27,539 --> 00:02:29,349
It's already been years.

61
00:02:29,810 --> 00:02:33,020
She said she was going to go to work like usual...

62
00:02:33,180 --> 00:02:35,680
- and pack her stuff afterward. - Really?

63
00:02:35,680 --> 00:02:37,490
I went with her to celebrate it...

64
00:02:37,490 --> 00:02:40,719
and to make it a lasting memory for her.

65
00:02:40,990 --> 00:02:42,990
I'm spending the whole day for my mother...

66
00:02:43,129 --> 00:02:45,930
and to throw a surprise party for my mother's retirement.

67
00:02:46,060 --> 00:02:47,729
I'm going to spend it well.

68
00:02:48,229 --> 00:02:49,469
The last day of her work.

69
00:02:49,469 --> 00:02:51,599
How must the last day of work feel?

70
00:02:51,599 --> 00:02:53,199
- She worked there for so long. - She worked there her whole life.

71
00:02:53,199 --> 00:02:54,340
- She did. - She worked there...

72
00:02:54,340 --> 00:02:55,370
- That's right. - for more than 30 years.

73
00:02:55,370 --> 00:02:56,870
- Yes, for 36 years. - That's really amazing.

74
00:02:56,870 --> 00:02:58,210
It couldn't have hit her...

75
00:02:58,210 --> 00:02:59,539
- on the day. - Right.

76
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:01,180
It must've felt odd.

77
00:03:02,240 --> 00:03:04,509
(He's still lazing around in bed.)

78
00:03:04,509 --> 00:03:06,849
I need to go now.

79
00:03:06,849 --> 00:03:09,919
It's your mother's last day of work, so why are you still in bed?

80
00:03:10,020 --> 00:03:11,050
What...

81
00:03:11,319 --> 00:03:12,719
Why are you suddenly mad?

82
00:03:12,719 --> 00:03:14,659
- What made him mad? - It's still early.

83
00:03:14,659 --> 00:03:15,759
- Your mother is working hard, - But...

84
00:03:15,759 --> 00:03:17,060
- and it's her last day. - I mean...

85
00:03:17,060 --> 00:03:18,330
I'm suddenly mad.

86
00:03:19,030 --> 00:03:20,360
Why are you so mad?

87
00:03:20,360 --> 00:03:22,900
- I went there to celebrate. - Exactly.

88
00:03:23,699 --> 00:03:24,729
(Get up now.)

89
00:03:25,030 --> 00:03:26,139
No.

90
00:03:26,139 --> 00:03:27,199
(I can't be in bed.)

91
00:03:28,000 --> 00:03:30,139
(He hurriedly leaves the bedroom.)

92
00:03:32,069 --> 00:03:33,180
How do I turn the lights on again?

93
00:03:34,680 --> 00:03:35,949
Gosh, it's so bright.

94
00:03:38,479 --> 00:03:40,979
(KEY enters the kitchen as soon as he gets up.)

95
00:03:41,819 --> 00:03:43,889
(Let's see.)

96
00:03:45,020 --> 00:03:46,960
(Opening)

97
00:03:46,960 --> 00:03:48,490
(Closing)

98
00:03:49,259 --> 00:03:52,090
(He rummages through everywhere and checks their stuff.)

99
00:03:54,060 --> 00:03:56,169
(Rattling)

100
00:03:56,400 --> 00:03:57,530
With this...

101
00:03:57,530 --> 00:03:58,770
(He takes out a pot.)

102
00:03:59,800 --> 00:04:01,840
Will this be big enough?

103
00:04:04,110 --> 00:04:06,610
(Let's use a bigger pot.)

104
00:04:06,610 --> 00:04:08,680
- He must be cooking something. - A lunch box?

105
00:04:09,009 --> 00:04:10,050
You said...

106
00:04:10,050 --> 00:04:11,080
- your mom was a bad cook. - Yes.

107
00:04:11,080 --> 00:04:12,180
(Is he cooking for his mother?)

108
00:04:12,650 --> 00:04:15,050
(Grabbing something from the utility room.)

109
00:04:17,150 --> 00:04:18,219
What's that?

110
00:04:18,719 --> 00:04:20,719
(This time, he opens their fridge.)

111
00:04:21,759 --> 00:04:23,830
Now you're a certified Craftsman Cook in Western food.

112
00:04:23,830 --> 00:04:26,430
- Are you making an omelet? - No. There's no way.

113
00:04:27,600 --> 00:04:28,730
(The dish he's best at after omelet)

114
00:04:28,730 --> 00:04:29,769
(All right.)

115
00:04:30,230 --> 00:04:32,230
(He moves something heavy.)

116
00:04:33,040 --> 00:04:34,040
- Is it octopus? - I couldn't make her...

117
00:04:34,040 --> 00:04:35,199
- Is it oyster? - an ordinary dish.

118
00:04:35,600 --> 00:04:37,339
What is it? It must be something in season.

119
00:04:37,339 --> 00:04:38,509
I'm curious.

120
00:04:38,670 --> 00:04:39,980
(Pouring)

121
00:04:39,980 --> 00:04:41,439
- It must be clams. - It sounds like clams.

122
00:04:41,439 --> 00:04:42,540
- I know. - Or abalones?

123
00:04:43,209 --> 00:04:44,779
- Oysters? They're crabs? - They're crabs.

124
00:04:44,779 --> 00:04:48,649
I think I've mentioned this while talking with the members.

125
00:04:48,980 --> 00:04:52,720
My grandma used to make this really often when I was young.

126
00:04:53,189 --> 00:04:55,560
It's braised crab in soy sauce.

127
00:04:56,829 --> 00:05:00,060
(KEY was reminded of something upon seeing Na Rae's kimchi.)

128
00:05:00,430 --> 00:05:02,259
(It's been over 10 years since Grandma passed.)

129
00:05:03,100 --> 00:05:05,269
(He really craved her braised crab in soy sauce.)

130
00:05:05,870 --> 00:05:08,339
(He spent the whole day finding the right taste.)

131
00:05:09,339 --> 00:05:11,740
(Using the seed soy sauce that's been boiled as a base)

132
00:05:12,709 --> 00:05:15,610
(He found the correct recipe after twists and turns.)

133
00:05:15,810 --> 00:05:17,379
- That's right. - I thought...

134
00:05:17,379 --> 00:05:19,420
everyone in the country ate it.

135
00:05:19,620 --> 00:05:21,050
It was introduced, but no one knew.

136
00:05:21,350 --> 00:05:22,519
You'd never heard of it, right?

137
00:05:22,519 --> 00:05:23,620
- I hadn't. - No.

138
00:05:23,620 --> 00:05:26,089
- There's such food. - Yes, there's such food.

139
00:05:26,089 --> 00:05:27,459
- I've never heard of braised crab. - Right.

140
00:05:27,459 --> 00:05:29,930
I thought my mom and dad would really love it...

141
00:05:29,930 --> 00:05:31,759
if I made it for them.

142
00:05:31,959 --> 00:05:35,029
After eating it together, I went to school, and Mom went to work.

143
00:05:35,459 --> 00:05:37,430
We have memories of that.

144
00:05:37,430 --> 00:05:40,069
I thought she'd be reminded of the old days...

145
00:05:40,240 --> 00:05:42,439
if I made her Grandma's food.

146
00:05:42,439 --> 00:05:43,569
(KEY prepares braised crab in soy sauce.)

147
00:05:43,839 --> 00:05:44,839
(Opening)

148
00:05:44,839 --> 00:05:46,310
Gosh, they're still alive!

149
00:05:46,480 --> 00:05:47,810
(Surprised by the squirming live crabs)

150
00:05:47,810 --> 00:05:49,009
They're live blue crabs.

151
00:05:50,050 --> 00:05:51,350
(Fresh)

152
00:05:53,079 --> 00:05:55,819
- Gosh, they're plump. - It's not easy.

153
00:05:55,819 --> 00:05:57,319
Of course not.

154
00:05:58,319 --> 00:05:59,319
(Clenching his teeth)

155
00:05:59,319 --> 00:06:00,990
That's it. You're doing well.

156
00:06:01,990 --> 00:06:04,029
Let's leave this unconscious.

157
00:06:05,589 --> 00:06:07,160
Must you use live blue crabs...

158
00:06:07,160 --> 00:06:08,230
- for that dish? - Yes, you must.

159
00:06:09,129 --> 00:06:10,230
You must use live blue crabs.

160
00:06:11,269 --> 00:06:13,670
(The next step is important.)

161
00:06:15,500 --> 00:06:17,410
I brought the seed soy sauce from home.

162
00:06:17,410 --> 00:06:19,839
(Pouring the seed soy sauce he brought from home)

163
00:06:20,810 --> 00:06:23,310
It's the same seed soy sauce I used to make the same dish.

164
00:06:23,850 --> 00:06:26,350
So it was used to braise crabs.

165
00:06:26,350 --> 00:06:28,819
- You stored it? - I stored it.

166
00:06:28,819 --> 00:06:31,019
- That's amazing. - You pour it...

167
00:06:31,019 --> 00:06:33,620
when you make a new batch. Then you store the sauce again...

168
00:06:33,620 --> 00:06:34,860
to make another batch.

169
00:06:34,860 --> 00:06:36,129
You must pack that first when you move.

170
00:06:36,389 --> 00:06:37,560
Of course. Seed soy sauce...

171
00:06:37,560 --> 00:06:38,589
- is really important. - He must pack it first.

172
00:06:38,589 --> 00:06:39,759
- There... - It'll be with him forever.

173
00:06:39,759 --> 00:06:41,730
There used to be a seed soy sauce thief in old restaurants.

174
00:06:41,730 --> 00:06:43,870
- Of course. - But it'll be gone once you eat it.

175
00:06:43,870 --> 00:06:45,500
- You don't throw the old one. - You boil it again.

176
00:06:45,500 --> 00:06:46,540
You keep boiling it for decades.

177
00:06:47,069 --> 00:06:48,170
- I see. - The taste changes completely.

178
00:06:48,170 --> 00:06:49,970
- You can't even buy it. - You have a seed lunchbox.

179
00:06:49,970 --> 00:06:51,509
- Seed soy sauce... - A seed lunchbox?

180
00:06:51,569 --> 00:06:55,040
(The expert in seed fried rice, Seed84)

181
00:06:56,009 --> 00:06:57,350
(Seed fried rice is my secret technique.)

182
00:06:58,250 --> 00:07:00,019
(He seasons it in earnest.)

183
00:07:00,120 --> 00:07:01,649
Adding minced garlic.

184
00:07:02,850 --> 00:07:04,120
You're adding more soy sauce?

185
00:07:04,120 --> 00:07:05,420
- We can't even copy it... - That's the secret...

186
00:07:05,420 --> 00:07:06,990
- because we don't have the seed. - of making the sauce.

187
00:07:07,160 --> 00:07:10,060
You can use this recipe when you first make the sauce.

188
00:07:10,189 --> 00:07:12,430
With 1.5 portions of water, add 1 portion of starch syrup.

189
00:07:12,529 --> 00:07:15,660
With a ratio like that, add 0.2 portions of plum extract.

190
00:07:15,660 --> 00:07:17,269
And 0.2 portions of fish sauce.

191
00:07:17,769 --> 00:07:20,470
- Oh, you must add vinegar too. - Oh, you add sand eel sauce.

192
00:07:20,470 --> 00:07:21,470
- That must be good. - That must be good.

193
00:07:21,470 --> 00:07:22,639
- Add lots of chili powder. - Chili powder.

194
00:07:22,639 --> 00:07:23,769
(Then add a few rounds of savory sesame oil.)

195
00:07:23,769 --> 00:07:25,970
- It needs sesame oil too. - That's unique.

196
00:07:26,509 --> 00:07:28,040
But that's... It looks delicious.

197
00:07:28,040 --> 00:07:29,180
It must be delicious to braise tofu with it.

198
00:07:29,180 --> 00:07:32,050
You must add a lot of water to keep the crabs completely soaked.

199
00:07:32,550 --> 00:07:34,149
- You add potato too? - Yes.

200
00:07:34,319 --> 00:07:36,449
- That's really unique. - Is it a Daegu dish?

201
00:07:36,449 --> 00:07:37,589
No, there's no such dish.

202
00:07:38,589 --> 00:07:41,389
(Braised crab in soy sauce that only KEY's family enjoys)

203
00:07:43,490 --> 00:07:44,490
(In the soy sauce with various condiments...)

204
00:07:44,490 --> 00:07:45,629
Bye.

205
00:07:45,730 --> 00:07:47,399
So you boil it down like that.

206
00:07:47,529 --> 00:07:49,529
- That's really unique. - What is...

207
00:07:49,529 --> 00:07:51,629
(Adding blue crabs one by one)

208
00:07:52,029 --> 00:07:54,000
- I wonder how it tastes. - I'm really curious too.

209
00:07:55,100 --> 00:07:57,310
(Adding lots of onions that have been cut)

210
00:07:58,170 --> 00:08:00,139
(Adding potatoes on top)

211
00:08:00,610 --> 00:08:02,240
(Adding his love too)

212
00:08:03,949 --> 00:08:05,009
That's it.

213
00:08:05,350 --> 00:08:06,879
Ring the alarm in ten minutes.

214
00:08:08,319 --> 00:08:10,649
- That's it? - Yes. I just need to boil it.

215
00:08:10,649 --> 00:08:12,050
- That's really simple. - It is.

216
00:08:12,050 --> 00:08:13,589
- Boil it for exactly ten minutes? - Yes.

217
00:08:14,189 --> 00:08:16,160
(Exhaling)

218
00:08:20,029 --> 00:08:21,259
Gosh, there's a lot to do.

219
00:08:25,230 --> 00:08:26,300
Goodness.

220
00:08:26,670 --> 00:08:28,069
- You're throwing a party at home? - Yes.

221
00:08:28,069 --> 00:08:30,439
- We're having it at home. - Your mother would love it.

222
00:08:30,709 --> 00:08:32,370
I got tape too.

223
00:08:34,240 --> 00:08:35,540
I got lots of stuff.

224
00:08:35,540 --> 00:08:36,750
(Opening gently)

225
00:08:38,410 --> 00:08:39,649
(Grabbing)

226
00:08:41,549 --> 00:08:43,289
(Mother I'm proud of)

227
00:08:43,289 --> 00:08:44,919
- Gosh. - He's such a good son.

228
00:08:44,919 --> 00:08:46,090
He's a really good son.

229
00:08:46,789 --> 00:08:48,019
I've never done that for my parents.

230
00:08:48,120 --> 00:08:49,320
- No. - She would love it.

231
00:08:49,659 --> 00:08:53,000
My mother loves cute things like that.

232
00:08:53,059 --> 00:08:56,899
That's why I wanted to make a figure for her.

233
00:08:57,070 --> 00:08:59,269
- It's like a trophy. - That'd be lovely.

234
00:08:59,269 --> 00:09:00,340
He's such a good son.

235
00:09:00,570 --> 00:09:01,870
(Mother I'm proud of, Kim Sun Hee)

236
00:09:03,409 --> 00:09:05,370
(The good son, KEY, begins decorating the house.)

237
00:09:05,370 --> 00:09:06,779
(Determined)

238
00:09:08,409 --> 00:09:09,950
(Let's speed it up.)

239
00:09:10,850 --> 00:09:13,049
(His eyes and hands are busy moving.)

240
00:09:14,879 --> 00:09:17,090
Oh, he's blowing balloons one by one.

241
00:09:17,090 --> 00:09:18,149
That's a lot of work.

242
00:09:18,149 --> 00:09:19,149
(He makes a balloon bouquet without moving or resting.)

243
00:09:20,620 --> 00:09:22,590
(Ringing)

244
00:09:24,460 --> 00:09:25,590
You should turn it off.

245
00:09:26,330 --> 00:09:28,629
Okay, I'm coming.

246
00:09:29,600 --> 00:09:31,330
(Hurrying)

247
00:09:32,570 --> 00:09:33,870
(One is done.)

248
00:09:35,799 --> 00:09:37,840
(A silver curtain to add to the party vibe)

249
00:09:41,139 --> 00:09:42,580
(Covered in sweat)

250
00:09:42,580 --> 00:09:44,149
- He sweats a lot. - That's right.

251
00:09:44,379 --> 00:09:46,950
- My parents' house... - It's hot.

252
00:09:46,950 --> 00:09:49,549
is really hot as though it's heated with ondol.

253
00:09:50,019 --> 00:09:51,350
- It's boiling hot. - You know what I mean, right?

254
00:09:51,350 --> 00:09:53,190
They increase the temperature even more when I'm coming.

255
00:09:53,389 --> 00:09:55,460
They wanted their son to stay warm.

256
00:09:56,860 --> 00:09:58,860
(Working hard to pay back his mom's warm love)

257
00:10:00,500 --> 00:10:04,929
(Pressing hard to fix it so it wouldn't fall)

258
00:10:06,340 --> 00:10:07,500
(Hanging a banner)

259
00:10:07,500 --> 00:10:08,669
That's nice.

260
00:10:09,340 --> 00:10:10,539
Nice!

261
00:10:10,539 --> 00:10:12,440
- You need a banner like that. - Yes, it's a must.

262
00:10:12,440 --> 00:10:14,340
It's a must. My mom keeps all those.

263
00:10:14,340 --> 00:10:16,210
Right. She seemed to like it.

264
00:10:16,649 --> 00:10:18,149
- Your mother would love it. - You can't leave it out.

265
00:10:18,149 --> 00:10:19,750
(We root for the 2nd chapter of your shining life.)

266
00:10:20,580 --> 00:10:21,720
(Adding more decorations on top of the banner)

267
00:10:21,720 --> 00:10:22,750
That's nice.

268
00:10:22,950 --> 00:10:24,220
The hearts are cute.

269
00:10:24,419 --> 00:10:26,789
How can you not feel embarrassed and do such things well?

270
00:10:26,789 --> 00:10:28,860
I can always do it.

271
00:10:29,120 --> 00:10:30,360
It's all set.

272
00:10:30,429 --> 00:10:31,590
(We root for the 2nd chapter of your shining life.)

273
00:10:31,590 --> 00:10:32,759
That's so cute.

274
00:10:34,100 --> 00:10:36,899
(Bringing the balloon bouquets he made in advance)

275
00:10:38,169 --> 00:10:40,070
(Placing the appreciation plaque and her figure)

276
00:10:40,669 --> 00:10:41,899
It's pretty.

277
00:10:42,970 --> 00:10:45,509
(Great. This is perfect.)

278
00:10:47,179 --> 00:10:48,710
(Sighing)

279
00:10:48,879 --> 00:10:50,009
Gosh, it's so hectic.

280
00:10:50,850 --> 00:10:52,980
- It's so hectic. - It looks exhausting already.

281
00:10:56,149 --> 00:10:59,149
(He got changed in no time.)

282
00:11:00,889 --> 00:11:02,820
(He's leaving the house in the midst of party preparation?)

283
00:11:02,820 --> 00:11:04,159
Where is he going?

284
00:11:10,830 --> 00:11:11,899
Taxi?

285
00:11:13,340 --> 00:11:15,240
- Hello. - Hello.

286
00:11:18,639 --> 00:11:20,080
(Curious)

287
00:11:21,779 --> 00:11:23,909
(Entering somewhere)

288
00:11:25,409 --> 00:11:27,120
Thank you.

289
00:11:27,750 --> 00:11:29,820
Goodness.

290
00:11:30,019 --> 00:11:31,220
Where is that?

291
00:11:32,149 --> 00:11:36,259
The place I went to is where my mother worked for 36 years.

292
00:11:36,259 --> 00:11:37,289
It's Kyungpook National University Hospital.

293
00:11:37,289 --> 00:11:38,360
Gosh, for 36 years?

294
00:11:38,360 --> 00:11:40,230
The hospital is huge.

295
00:11:40,399 --> 00:11:41,460
My mother...

296
00:11:42,059 --> 00:11:44,470
gave birth in her first workplace.

297
00:11:44,470 --> 00:11:46,129
She also got surgery there when she was ill.

298
00:11:46,129 --> 00:11:48,899
Every moment of her life is in those 36 years.

299
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:50,309
The first and last.

300
00:11:50,309 --> 00:11:51,840
That's how it was like for old people.

301
00:11:52,139 --> 00:11:54,080
- They'd work for just one place. - That's right.

302
00:11:54,179 --> 00:11:55,340
That's amazing.

303
00:11:56,480 --> 00:11:58,210
(KEY got out in front of the hospital.)

304
00:12:02,850 --> 00:12:05,389
- Hey, Ki Bum. - Mom, I'm here.

305
00:12:05,389 --> 00:12:07,360
- Yes. - Where should I go to?

306
00:12:07,720 --> 00:12:12,129
There's Building One on the rightmost side.

307
00:12:12,460 --> 00:12:15,759
- The Cancer Center? - Yes. Come to the second floor.

308
00:12:15,759 --> 00:12:17,600
The second floor? Okay.

309
00:12:17,600 --> 00:12:18,600
- All right. - Okay.

310
00:12:18,600 --> 00:12:21,440
She used to work in the main building when I was young,

311
00:12:21,840 --> 00:12:23,139
so I'm really familiar with it.

312
00:12:23,139 --> 00:12:25,970
But she moved to that building after I grew up.

313
00:12:27,909 --> 00:12:30,279
Kyungpook National University Hospital...

314
00:12:30,279 --> 00:12:32,210
is my mom's first and last workplace.

315
00:12:32,450 --> 00:12:34,049
She worked there as a nurse.

316
00:12:35,379 --> 00:12:37,120
(After the years where there were 3 shifts...)

317
00:12:37,120 --> 00:12:38,750
My gosh. That's the old nurse uniform.

318
00:12:38,750 --> 00:12:40,419
- It's really long ago. - Oh, my.

319
00:12:40,490 --> 00:12:41,889
She used to work for the auditing department...

320
00:12:41,889 --> 00:12:43,129
and was a head nurse.

321
00:12:43,129 --> 00:12:45,429
Now, she's the chief nurse.

322
00:12:46,860 --> 00:12:47,929
(She received a commendation from the Ministry of Health and Welfare.)

323
00:12:47,929 --> 00:12:49,059
A commendation?

324
00:12:50,470 --> 00:12:53,470
I'd always tag along with my dad...

325
00:12:53,470 --> 00:12:56,340
on her way to work. We'd also wait for her when she was done with work.

326
00:12:56,370 --> 00:12:58,340
I have so many memories like those.

327
00:12:58,370 --> 00:12:59,909
And I really loved that time.

328
00:13:00,679 --> 00:13:03,379
I thought it'd be meaningful to be with her...

329
00:13:03,379 --> 00:13:04,779
on her last day of work.

330
00:13:04,779 --> 00:13:06,779
I strongly wanted to be there for her.

331
00:13:07,720 --> 00:13:09,220
I wanted to be with her when she was done with work...

332
00:13:09,220 --> 00:13:10,649
rather than when she was going to work.

333
00:13:10,649 --> 00:13:13,389
- That'd feel more empty. - Right. That's more appropriate.

334
00:13:13,460 --> 00:13:16,320
Hyun Moo, you're planning to become a good son in five years.

335
00:13:16,490 --> 00:13:18,990
I'm not even sure when their last day of work was.

336
00:13:19,159 --> 00:13:21,230
- We were each... - What about when they got off work?

337
00:13:21,559 --> 00:13:24,000
Get off work? I didn't even know where they worked.

338
00:13:25,230 --> 00:13:26,600
- So... - We fended for ourselves.

339
00:13:26,600 --> 00:13:27,940
- Wait. - You're such a good son.

340
00:13:27,940 --> 00:13:29,039
You must know...

341
00:13:29,039 --> 00:13:30,110
- that this year is the last year. - What a good son.

342
00:13:30,110 --> 00:13:31,340
- Okay. - This year is the last one.

343
00:13:31,340 --> 00:13:32,769
- A year is quite long. - It's the last year.

344
00:13:32,840 --> 00:13:35,179
My mother worked her whole life...

345
00:13:35,279 --> 00:13:38,250
at an affiliated kindergarten.

346
00:13:38,750 --> 00:13:40,250
I never got to do something like that for her.

347
00:13:40,250 --> 00:13:41,620
Be honest. You didn't know when her last day was, right?

348
00:13:41,919 --> 00:13:44,519
I mean, I noticed...

349
00:13:44,519 --> 00:13:46,250
- she had retired already one day. - One day?

350
00:13:46,250 --> 00:13:48,289
- You and I are the same. - What are you saying?

351
00:13:48,289 --> 00:13:49,389
"Mom, aren't you going to work?"

352
00:13:49,389 --> 00:13:50,490
(Sorry. I realized it too late, Mom.)

353
00:13:50,490 --> 00:13:51,529
(Let's be a good son from now.)

354
00:13:52,929 --> 00:13:55,500
(Walking over to be with his mom on her last day of work)

355
00:13:55,860 --> 00:13:57,500
(Daegu, North Gyeongsang Area Cancer Center)

356
00:13:57,500 --> 00:13:58,600
Gosh, it's huge.

357
00:13:58,799 --> 00:14:00,200
It really is huge.

358
00:14:01,240 --> 00:14:02,299
Here it is.

359
00:14:02,639 --> 00:14:04,269
(Cancer Management Department, Reception)

360
00:14:04,269 --> 00:14:05,809
- Hello. - You're here.

361
00:14:05,809 --> 00:14:07,840
- Hello. - Hi!

362
00:14:07,840 --> 00:14:08,980
- Mom! - Yes.

363
00:14:08,980 --> 00:14:10,179
(They're glad to see each other.)

364
00:14:10,179 --> 00:14:11,309
Gosh, Mother.

365
00:14:11,509 --> 00:14:12,610
I mean...

366
00:14:13,179 --> 00:14:14,580
- What's all this? - Hello.

367
00:14:15,049 --> 00:14:18,019
- What's this? - I brought it for the team.

368
00:14:18,350 --> 00:14:19,750
- Really? - Her pitch is higher.

369
00:14:19,750 --> 00:14:21,259
- Gosh, put it up here. - Right.

370
00:14:22,090 --> 00:14:24,360
- Goodness. Say hello to them. - Goodness.

371
00:14:24,490 --> 00:14:26,799
- They're my colleagues. - Hello.

372
00:14:27,230 --> 00:14:28,730
They're so excited.

373
00:14:28,799 --> 00:14:30,629
Hello. Is that your seat?

374
00:14:30,629 --> 00:14:32,000
Yes, this is my seat.

375
00:14:32,000 --> 00:14:33,200
You're all working so hard.

376
00:14:33,370 --> 00:14:35,139
This is my seat.

377
00:14:35,440 --> 00:14:37,070
There's nothing much to pack.

378
00:14:38,409 --> 00:14:40,210
Isn't the seat where you can see the whole room...

379
00:14:40,210 --> 00:14:41,710
for someone in the highest position?

380
00:14:41,710 --> 00:14:43,110
- That's right. - That's right. On the inside.

381
00:14:43,309 --> 00:14:45,610
I saw Mom's seat as soon as I opened the door.

382
00:14:45,980 --> 00:14:49,549
I realized that my mom made it to a high position.

383
00:14:49,649 --> 00:14:51,750
I could see how she made it...

384
00:14:51,750 --> 00:14:53,789
to the final position of her career.

385
00:14:54,389 --> 00:14:56,059
She wasn't in such a position...

386
00:14:56,059 --> 00:14:57,360
- when I first went there. - That's right.

387
00:14:57,490 --> 00:15:01,330
I always visited my mom's office once or twice a year.

388
00:15:01,330 --> 00:15:02,529
Since I was young.

389
00:15:02,799 --> 00:15:06,570
Her senior colleagues would tell me that she's working hard.

390
00:15:06,639 --> 00:15:09,799
We used to use landline, so I'd call and ask for my mom.

391
00:15:09,799 --> 00:15:12,039
- That's how we used to call. - That's right.

392
00:15:12,169 --> 00:15:15,279
The one who picked up the phone would compliment my mom in person.

393
00:15:15,279 --> 00:15:17,309
They'd greet me happily.

394
00:15:17,309 --> 00:15:19,149
That's the culture we had when I was young.

395
00:15:20,779 --> 00:15:23,419
Let's go. We'll be in the meeting room.

396
00:15:24,049 --> 00:15:26,289
- Goodness. - It must be heavy.

397
00:15:26,289 --> 00:15:27,759
(Heading to the meeting room, following his mom's lead)

398
00:15:28,059 --> 00:15:29,759
Ta-da.

399
00:15:30,190 --> 00:15:32,330
You brought me here to show this to me, right?

400
00:15:34,059 --> 00:15:36,429
(A banner to celebrate her retirement)

401
00:15:36,429 --> 00:15:37,929
She must be popular among her colleagues.

402
00:15:37,929 --> 00:15:39,269
Yes, she is.

403
00:15:39,269 --> 00:15:41,240
(We root for the new beginning of Ms. Kim Sun Hee.)

404
00:15:41,240 --> 00:15:42,899
What's with this photo edit?

405
00:15:43,269 --> 00:15:44,740
It's your life-size cutout.

406
00:15:45,139 --> 00:15:46,809
- Does it actually exist? - It's you?

407
00:15:46,809 --> 00:15:48,110
- You're there too? - Yes.

408
00:15:48,110 --> 00:15:49,210
- What is this? - Oh, my.

409
00:15:49,210 --> 00:15:50,750
- That's so witty of them. - It was my birthday.

410
00:15:50,750 --> 00:15:52,009
- My colleagues... - Oh, it was your birthday.

411
00:15:52,009 --> 00:15:53,519
threw me a party.

412
00:15:54,220 --> 00:15:56,250
How should I give these out?

413
00:15:56,250 --> 00:15:57,919
(By the way, how should I give these out?)

414
00:16:03,889 --> 00:16:05,590
Why did you bring so much?

415
00:16:05,889 --> 00:16:07,029
What is it?

416
00:16:08,330 --> 00:16:10,629
- You signed all of them? - Gosh.

417
00:16:11,070 --> 00:16:12,070
(Surprised)

418
00:16:12,070 --> 00:16:13,100
Oh, my.

419
00:16:13,299 --> 00:16:16,000
(A bag of gifts he prepared for his mom's colleagues)

420
00:16:17,909 --> 00:16:19,210
Oh, my gosh.

421
00:16:19,970 --> 00:16:22,580
(There's more gift?)

422
00:16:24,110 --> 00:16:25,279
(Exclaiming)

423
00:16:25,279 --> 00:16:26,850
Isn't it so moving?

424
00:16:26,850 --> 00:16:27,950
What is it?

425
00:16:28,480 --> 00:16:30,149
What is this?

426
00:16:30,889 --> 00:16:32,919
(The second gift is a ballpoint pen.)

427
00:16:33,019 --> 00:16:35,159
Nurses use ballpoint pens a lot.

428
00:16:35,159 --> 00:16:37,690
No matter how digitalized it has become,

429
00:16:37,929 --> 00:16:39,360
they use ballpoint pens a lot.

430
00:16:39,590 --> 00:16:41,429
So I prepared pens with a message engraved.

431
00:16:41,429 --> 00:16:42,759
Oh, they're for her colleagues?

432
00:16:42,759 --> 00:16:45,200
- Yes. - My, how considerate of you.

433
00:16:45,200 --> 00:16:47,100
"Please brag to others that I gave this to you."

434
00:16:47,100 --> 00:16:48,539
I carved in a message to thank them.

435
00:16:48,970 --> 00:16:50,070
- My name should be included... - He's so considerate.

436
00:16:50,070 --> 00:16:51,610
for them to brag.

437
00:16:51,610 --> 00:16:52,610
That's right.

438
00:16:52,610 --> 00:16:53,610
- You should show it. - Of course.

439
00:16:53,610 --> 00:16:55,039
What is this?

440
00:16:56,340 --> 00:16:57,809
You carved it on all of them?

441
00:16:58,379 --> 00:16:59,679
Of course.

442
00:16:59,679 --> 00:17:00,720
You did well.

443
00:17:00,950 --> 00:17:03,179
Gosh, it's rewarding to have a son.

444
00:17:03,320 --> 00:17:04,590
(Feeling proud)

445
00:17:05,250 --> 00:17:07,059
How should I hand them out?

446
00:17:07,289 --> 00:17:09,019
Guys, you should...

447
00:17:09,019 --> 00:17:11,230
Come on in.

448
00:17:11,230 --> 00:17:12,730
(Surprised)

449
00:17:12,860 --> 00:17:14,559
(Her colleagues are gathered in front of the door.)

450
00:17:14,730 --> 00:17:16,799
- He brought these for us. - I wanted to give them out.

451
00:17:16,799 --> 00:17:18,299
He's our center head.

452
00:17:18,299 --> 00:17:20,200
- Oh, you're the center head. Hello. - Can I shake his hand?

453
00:17:20,200 --> 00:17:21,970
It's nice to meet you.

454
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:24,069
He's the head of the cancer center.

455
00:17:24,170 --> 00:17:25,970
- Oh, my. - It's my album.

456
00:17:25,970 --> 00:17:26,980
(Giving a gift to him right away)

457
00:17:26,980 --> 00:17:28,809
By the way, sir...

458
00:17:28,809 --> 00:17:30,880
- There's something he must get. - there's something I must get.

459
00:17:30,880 --> 00:17:32,109
- What is it? - My daughter...

460
00:17:32,109 --> 00:17:33,619
Oh, she bought it.

461
00:17:33,619 --> 00:17:35,880
- Sign it here. - I should sign it?

462
00:17:35,880 --> 00:17:37,950
She told me to get your autograph. Then she'd study hard.

463
00:17:37,950 --> 00:17:39,750
(Signing his autograph without holding back)

464
00:17:40,589 --> 00:17:42,519
He's done it a lot.

465
00:17:43,019 --> 00:17:45,059
- Your mother must be so proud. - Thank you.

466
00:17:45,059 --> 00:17:46,589
She even bought the album?

467
00:17:46,589 --> 00:17:48,029
- Thank you. - Thank you.

468
00:17:48,029 --> 00:17:49,859
- Be healthy. - Thank you for everything.

469
00:17:50,059 --> 00:17:51,730
- Here. Please take them. - My son signed all of them.

470
00:17:51,730 --> 00:17:53,500
- Really? Thank you. - I already signed them.

471
00:17:53,500 --> 00:17:55,940
It's nice to have a successful son.

472
00:17:55,940 --> 00:17:57,109
- He's her pride. - I'm even more thankful.

473
00:17:57,109 --> 00:17:59,240
- Thank you. - Thank you.

474
00:17:59,640 --> 00:18:01,609
- You're all working so hard. - Thank you.

475
00:18:01,940 --> 00:18:04,309
- Sure. - You really made her proud.

476
00:18:04,650 --> 00:18:07,319
- I rarely have a chance like that. - Thank you for your hard work.

477
00:18:07,680 --> 00:18:09,119
(Smiling broadly thanks to her son)

478
00:18:09,450 --> 00:18:11,190
- Let's take a photo to wrap up. - Now...

479
00:18:11,190 --> 00:18:12,220
That's right.

480
00:18:12,390 --> 00:18:14,019
Should we take a photo with the white backdrop?

481
00:18:14,190 --> 00:18:15,460
Let's take it in front of that.

482
00:18:15,460 --> 00:18:17,359
- This? But it's... - Yes.

483
00:18:17,359 --> 00:18:19,329
Your life-size cutout was always with us,

484
00:18:19,329 --> 00:18:21,000
- but you're here yourself. - Let's take a photo with him.

485
00:18:22,299 --> 00:18:24,670
One, two, three.

486
00:18:25,599 --> 00:18:27,500
(A group photo with KEY in person)

487
00:18:29,500 --> 00:18:30,670
Let's go.

488
00:18:32,240 --> 00:18:34,809
(Grabbing his arm tight)

489
00:18:34,809 --> 00:18:37,880
There was an order of events my mother planned ahead.

490
00:18:38,450 --> 00:18:41,819
(My son feels especially reliable today.)

491
00:18:43,690 --> 00:18:45,049
(His mother enters the room first.)

492
00:18:45,049 --> 00:18:46,960
- Hello. - Hello.

493
00:18:47,460 --> 00:18:48,589
(Where is this?)

494
00:18:48,589 --> 00:18:51,160
- Hello. - Where is that?

495
00:18:51,490 --> 00:18:53,900
- Hello. - Oh, my. It's KEY.

496
00:18:54,700 --> 00:18:56,130
Gosh, department head.

497
00:18:56,130 --> 00:18:57,599
Hello.

498
00:18:57,599 --> 00:18:58,599
It's KEY.

499
00:18:58,630 --> 00:19:00,170
(It's the office of the nurse department head.)

500
00:19:00,170 --> 00:19:02,440
It's the nurse department where my mom used to work.

501
00:19:03,609 --> 00:19:05,869
- It's nice to meet you. - Hello.

502
00:19:05,869 --> 00:19:09,039
He came to see me as it's my last day of work.

503
00:19:09,210 --> 00:19:11,210
- How fascinating. - I'll give you my album.

504
00:19:11,549 --> 00:19:14,279
- I'm enjoying your shows a lot. - Thank you so much.

505
00:19:14,279 --> 00:19:15,480
(Giving her a gift with gratitude)

506
00:19:15,480 --> 00:19:17,490
- Thank you. - Thank you.

507
00:19:18,089 --> 00:19:19,619
They prepared something for you.

508
00:19:20,789 --> 00:19:22,960
- They got it for me last time. - Oh, my.

509
00:19:22,960 --> 00:19:25,490
- Great work. - Thank you.

510
00:19:25,490 --> 00:19:27,130
- Great work. - Thank you.

511
00:19:27,130 --> 00:19:28,160
(They even got her flowers.)

512
00:19:28,160 --> 00:19:29,930
- What are you doing? - I'm so grateful.

513
00:19:30,630 --> 00:19:32,430
You're getting many flowers today.

514
00:19:32,900 --> 00:19:34,400
What are you doing?

515
00:19:34,400 --> 00:19:37,009
How can she be congratulated by so many people?

516
00:19:37,109 --> 00:19:38,970
- It's really not easy. - Wait.

517
00:19:38,970 --> 00:19:40,910
(Then I should give something in return as her son.)

518
00:19:40,910 --> 00:19:42,980
- I'll give these out. - Come.

519
00:19:43,140 --> 00:19:44,349
- Thank you. - Thank you.

520
00:19:44,349 --> 00:19:46,349
- Come here, Sung Kyung. - Thank you.

521
00:19:46,509 --> 00:19:48,920
- Here you go. Thank you. - Thank you.

522
00:19:50,119 --> 00:19:52,049
I should've met you in person to congratulate.

523
00:19:52,490 --> 00:19:53,690
It's all right.

524
00:19:53,690 --> 00:19:55,690
- Thank you so much. - Not at all.

525
00:19:55,990 --> 00:19:57,660
Let's grab a meal outside.

526
00:19:57,660 --> 00:19:58,890
Hold on.

527
00:19:58,930 --> 00:20:00,529
(Approaching)

528
00:20:01,299 --> 00:20:02,859
I depended on you a lot.

529
00:20:02,859 --> 00:20:04,470
- I couldn't visit you more. - Of course.

530
00:20:05,130 --> 00:20:06,769
- Goodness. - I feel like crying.

531
00:20:06,769 --> 00:20:08,140
Don't.

532
00:20:08,240 --> 00:20:09,769
- Goodness. - Goodness.

533
00:20:10,470 --> 00:20:12,740
Thank you so much for your hard work.

534
00:20:13,440 --> 00:20:15,009
Gosh, don't cry.

535
00:20:15,279 --> 00:20:16,640
Oh, really?

536
00:20:16,640 --> 00:20:18,809
- You worked so hard. - Right.

537
00:20:19,250 --> 00:20:20,849
I'm grateful.

538
00:20:22,220 --> 00:20:23,220
Thank you for everything.

539
00:20:23,819 --> 00:20:24,819
- Thank you. - I feel the same way.

540
00:20:24,819 --> 00:20:26,920
I never even imagined this...

541
00:20:26,920 --> 00:20:28,059
and didn't expect it at all.

542
00:20:28,059 --> 00:20:29,190
(KEY is standing next to his mom.)

543
00:20:30,730 --> 00:20:33,490
Actually, working with the same people for 20 to 30 years...

544
00:20:33,660 --> 00:20:35,200
and suddenly saying goodbye overnight...

545
00:20:35,200 --> 00:20:37,529
must be a very sad thing for many people.

546
00:20:38,099 --> 00:20:39,670
In some ways, she has spent more time with them...

547
00:20:39,670 --> 00:20:41,099
- than her own family. - Of course.

548
00:20:41,400 --> 00:20:44,509
Seeing her feeling sad and shedding tears...

549
00:20:44,509 --> 00:20:46,170
made me so proud of her.

550
00:20:46,170 --> 00:20:48,440
I came to respect her once more today.

551
00:20:50,210 --> 00:20:52,579
- I'm so glad you went there. - Thank you.

552
00:20:52,950 --> 00:20:55,279
Then, I'll see you all later.

553
00:20:55,279 --> 00:20:57,289
- Okay. - Thank you for everything.

554
00:20:57,289 --> 00:20:59,319
So many people cried because of your retirement.

555
00:20:59,319 --> 00:21:01,390
I heard that so many people cried.

556
00:21:02,390 --> 00:21:03,420
Does it kind of feel like...

557
00:21:03,420 --> 00:21:04,430
when someone is discharged from military service?

558
00:21:04,430 --> 00:21:06,160
- I know, right? - If you act sloppy during service,

559
00:21:06,160 --> 00:21:07,460
- it gets awkward when you leave. - Right.

560
00:21:07,460 --> 00:21:09,359
- That's right. - And nobody cries for you.

561
00:21:09,359 --> 00:21:10,630
- Of course. - That's right.

562
00:21:10,630 --> 00:21:11,900
You say you won't let him slide if you see him outside.

563
00:21:11,900 --> 00:21:12,900
- Right. - Yes.

564
00:21:12,900 --> 00:21:14,000
Wait, Na Rae...

565
00:21:14,240 --> 00:21:15,839
(Wait, Na Rae?)

566
00:21:15,839 --> 00:21:16,900
Na Rae.

567
00:21:17,269 --> 00:21:18,740
- How do you know that? - Of course I know.

568
00:21:18,740 --> 00:21:20,579
- She's a sergeant major. - Yes, the sergeant major knows.

569
00:21:20,579 --> 00:21:21,640
Sergeant Major Park.

570
00:21:22,740 --> 00:21:24,950
It doesn't feel like your retirement party but mine.

571
00:21:26,380 --> 00:21:28,980
Still, it's so nice that my son is here.

572
00:21:30,220 --> 00:21:31,619
- Shall we go over there? - Yes.

573
00:21:34,359 --> 00:21:36,059
- You have a packed schedule. - Yes.

574
00:21:36,059 --> 00:21:37,230
- We had a lot to do. - Right.

575
00:21:37,230 --> 00:21:38,390
- You're making rounds to say bye. - Hello.

576
00:21:38,390 --> 00:21:40,190
Hello.

577
00:21:40,599 --> 00:21:41,599
(They arrive at a conference room?)

578
00:21:41,599 --> 00:21:42,930
Is the mic working?

579
00:21:42,930 --> 00:21:44,299
- A mic? - What is this?

580
00:21:44,730 --> 00:21:46,630
- Please sit here. - Yes, have a seat.

581
00:21:48,099 --> 00:21:49,099
What is it?

582
00:21:49,099 --> 00:21:50,299
- This is too... - We pulled it up a bit.

583
00:21:50,299 --> 00:21:52,240
I think this has gotten too big.

584
00:21:52,539 --> 00:21:53,640
Hello.

585
00:21:54,210 --> 00:21:55,240
Hey, what is this?

586
00:21:55,240 --> 00:21:56,640
- Should we sit here like this? - What is this?

587
00:21:56,640 --> 00:21:58,349
Why is there a photographer here?

588
00:21:59,250 --> 00:22:00,950
- Is the entire staff gathering? - This is the chairwoman.

589
00:22:00,950 --> 00:22:02,779
Oh, hello.

590
00:22:02,779 --> 00:22:04,150
This is the doctor. The professor.

591
00:22:04,150 --> 00:22:05,750
- Thank you for coming. - Thank you.

592
00:22:05,950 --> 00:22:07,359
- Congratulations. - My goodness.

593
00:22:07,660 --> 00:22:09,059
- I should shake hands too. - Yes.

594
00:22:09,359 --> 00:22:10,660
Oh, the hospital director is here.

595
00:22:10,960 --> 00:22:12,329
- All the higher-ups have come. - Thank you.

596
00:22:12,329 --> 00:22:14,029
- Even the director was there. - I've heard so much about you.

597
00:22:14,599 --> 00:22:16,400
This looks like "White Tower."

598
00:22:16,900 --> 00:22:18,829
Was this such an official event?

599
00:22:18,829 --> 00:22:19,930
(Donation Ceremony for Patients in Children's Hospital)

600
00:22:19,930 --> 00:22:21,599
Right, I saw this article.

601
00:22:21,740 --> 00:22:24,210
I wanted to make a meaningful donation...

602
00:22:24,210 --> 00:22:27,410
for my mom's retirement.

603
00:22:27,809 --> 00:22:29,680
But when I got there, they had prepared a ceremony.

604
00:22:29,680 --> 00:22:30,779
(Flustered)

605
00:22:30,779 --> 00:22:33,720
They had opened a donation ceremony.

606
00:22:34,119 --> 00:22:35,480
I was very surprised.

607
00:22:36,279 --> 00:22:37,789
- You should do it, though. - It must be kind of embarrassing.

608
00:22:38,589 --> 00:22:40,920
Gosh, the hospital director... The department head...

609
00:22:41,059 --> 00:22:42,119
My goodness.

610
00:22:42,119 --> 00:22:43,990
- It's like "White Tower." - Oh, my.

611
00:22:44,130 --> 00:22:45,490
- "SHINee, KEY." - "SHINee, KEY."

612
00:22:45,490 --> 00:22:46,789
- We did it like this back then too. - Did we?

613
00:22:46,789 --> 00:22:48,029
- Sure. - "Singer."

614
00:22:48,630 --> 00:22:49,930
- We did it like this back then too. - Look at me.

615
00:22:49,930 --> 00:22:51,730
- I was so embarrassed to sit there. - Sure.

616
00:22:51,730 --> 00:22:53,170
That's the most embarrassing.

617
00:22:53,470 --> 00:22:55,569
- Isn't the view great here? - Sure.

618
00:22:55,700 --> 00:22:56,970
This is the best seat.

619
00:22:57,410 --> 00:22:58,440
Hello, everyone.

620
00:22:58,440 --> 00:23:00,839
We will now begin the donation ceremony...

621
00:23:00,839 --> 00:23:04,250
for patients in the Children's Hospital with SHINee's KEY.

622
00:23:04,650 --> 00:23:05,650
(They begin with refined applause.)

623
00:23:05,650 --> 00:23:07,009
This is so official.

624
00:23:07,009 --> 00:23:08,450
(Shy)

625
00:23:08,619 --> 00:23:11,450
I will now introduce the guests in attendance today.

626
00:23:11,450 --> 00:23:13,049
- There is an order to the event. - You have to do this stuff.

627
00:23:13,049 --> 00:23:14,490
We have the director of...

628
00:23:14,490 --> 00:23:16,190
Kyungpook National University Chilgok Hospital.

629
00:23:16,690 --> 00:23:18,490
Even after all these years, these things stay the same.

630
00:23:19,089 --> 00:23:21,559
The Medical Director of the Children's Hospital is here.

631
00:23:22,000 --> 00:23:23,769
The Chief Secretary is here.

632
00:23:23,769 --> 00:23:25,569
The Director of Nursing is here.

633
00:23:25,900 --> 00:23:27,539
The Pharmacy Manager is here.

634
00:23:27,539 --> 00:23:28,940
- That's it. - You have to mention everyone.

635
00:23:28,940 --> 00:23:30,000
Yes.

636
00:23:30,569 --> 00:23:33,670
SHINee's KEY, Kim Ki Bum is here.

637
00:23:35,740 --> 00:23:37,579
(SHINee's KEY, Kim Ki Bum is here.)

638
00:23:37,579 --> 00:23:39,609
(Dizzy)

639
00:23:39,609 --> 00:23:41,019
Show them your "Lucifer" dance or something.

640
00:23:41,750 --> 00:23:43,549
- Right. - What are you saying?

641
00:23:44,220 --> 00:23:45,420
(Like this)

642
00:23:45,420 --> 00:23:46,950
You should've done something.

643
00:23:47,690 --> 00:23:48,960
You should've gone up to the front and danced.

644
00:23:48,960 --> 00:23:51,160
Next, we will have a congratulatory performance.

645
00:23:52,230 --> 00:23:55,059
(SHINee's KEY, Kim Ki Bum's "Lucifer")

646
00:23:55,059 --> 00:23:57,000
This was more embarrassing than an audition.

647
00:23:57,569 --> 00:23:58,630
Thank you.

648
00:23:58,630 --> 00:24:00,230
(He successfully finishes his solo performance.)

649
00:24:02,269 --> 00:24:04,910
And the mother of SHINee's KEY, Kim Ki Bum,

650
00:24:04,910 --> 00:24:06,839
Head Nurse Kim Sun Hee is here.

651
00:24:07,880 --> 00:24:09,839
(An enthusiastic cheer)

652
00:24:11,049 --> 00:24:14,549
Next, I will announce the donation history of SHINee's KEY.

653
00:24:15,150 --> 00:24:18,220
SHINee's KEY donated 10,000 dollars...

654
00:24:18,220 --> 00:24:20,049
on September 27, 2019.

655
00:24:20,049 --> 00:24:22,920
- My gosh. - You've been donating for a while.

656
00:24:22,920 --> 00:24:25,259
On March 22, 2024,

657
00:24:25,660 --> 00:24:28,660
he donated 864.4 dollars.

658
00:24:29,000 --> 00:24:31,730
When I received awards and stuff, I didn't take it and just donated.

659
00:24:32,069 --> 00:24:34,240
And today, he is donating 50,000 dollars...

660
00:24:34,240 --> 00:24:36,170
- Goodness. Good job. - He is donating 50,000 dollars...

661
00:24:36,539 --> 00:24:39,740
for patients in the Children's Hospital.

662
00:24:39,740 --> 00:24:42,609
- Good job. - Good job.

663
00:24:43,140 --> 00:24:44,549
I'm so proud.

664
00:24:44,549 --> 00:24:45,549
(Proud of him)

665
00:24:46,009 --> 00:24:48,680
We will now sign the donation contract.

666
00:24:49,450 --> 00:24:50,720
Is it usually like this?

667
00:24:50,720 --> 00:24:51,990
- It is. - Yes, you have to do it.

668
00:24:51,990 --> 00:24:53,650
There's a procedure to things.

669
00:24:54,160 --> 00:24:55,890
- You have to take a photo with it. - Of course.

670
00:24:56,259 --> 00:25:00,700
Next, with thanks to SHINee's KEY, Kim Ki Bum,

671
00:25:00,930 --> 00:25:02,829
the hospital director will say a few words.

672
00:25:02,829 --> 00:25:04,000
- Yes. - The director?

673
00:25:04,000 --> 00:25:05,170
The director will thank you with a speech?

674
00:25:05,170 --> 00:25:06,369
(The hospital director's speech)

675
00:25:07,130 --> 00:25:09,099
Thank you for visiting our hospital in person...

676
00:25:09,569 --> 00:25:11,809
for your mother's retirement...

677
00:25:11,809 --> 00:25:13,640
and showing us your warm sentiments.

678
00:25:13,940 --> 00:25:16,079
- We are so grateful. - Goodness, no.

679
00:25:16,380 --> 00:25:19,650
Actually, while I was talking to your mother,

680
00:25:19,650 --> 00:25:21,480
I heard that she had been working...

681
00:25:21,880 --> 00:25:25,789
at our hospital for 36 years and 8 months.

682
00:25:26,150 --> 00:25:28,990
In fact, she has been working here for longer than I have.

683
00:25:29,460 --> 00:25:33,230
As she worked here for so long and dedicated her youth,

684
00:25:33,230 --> 00:25:35,460
I'm sure she has gone through hardships as well.

685
00:25:35,559 --> 00:25:39,130
I was so grateful to her.

686
00:25:39,329 --> 00:25:43,700
Especially, her son is carrying on her dedication...

687
00:25:44,309 --> 00:25:46,539
and coming down here for the young patients...

688
00:25:46,539 --> 00:25:49,279
- to encourage them. When I heard, - Right.

689
00:25:49,279 --> 00:25:51,250
I wanted to express my gratitude.

690
00:25:51,250 --> 00:25:52,910
- I think your mom is tearing up. - I know.

691
00:25:52,910 --> 00:25:53,950
Thank you.

692
00:25:53,950 --> 00:25:57,079
(His mom's last day at work shines even brighter.)

693
00:25:57,220 --> 00:25:58,589
For my mom,

694
00:25:59,450 --> 00:26:04,089
it must've been such a long period that also went by in a flash.

695
00:26:04,759 --> 00:26:06,759
When she was working with children with cancer,

696
00:26:06,759 --> 00:26:09,599
she was so sincere to them. She went through so much heartache.

697
00:26:10,730 --> 00:26:12,069
I feel more relieved, actually.

698
00:26:12,670 --> 00:26:15,240
We will be rooting you on. Thank you.

699
00:26:16,369 --> 00:26:19,240
(Suddenly, they pass him a mic.)

700
00:26:19,240 --> 00:26:21,480
Next, we will ask SHINee's KEY, Kim Ki Bum...

701
00:26:21,880 --> 00:26:23,039
(What is it?)

702
00:26:23,039 --> 00:26:24,150
Is it a congratulatory performance?

703
00:26:24,150 --> 00:26:26,079
We will ask him to do a voice imitation.

704
00:26:26,549 --> 00:26:29,019
For a speech with a big round of applause.

705
00:26:29,150 --> 00:26:30,380
With a big round of applause.

706
00:26:32,490 --> 00:26:33,619
You didn't think of this, did you?

707
00:26:33,159 --> 00:26:35,460
Me? I just...

708
00:26:33,619 --> 00:26:34,890
(Expectant)

709
00:26:36,329 --> 00:26:37,430
(SHINee's KEY, Kim Ki Bum, you can do it!)

710
00:26:37,430 --> 00:26:38,430
(The camera is ready.)

711
00:26:38,960 --> 00:26:41,099
I have a loud voice, so...

712
00:26:41,099 --> 00:26:42,500
(SHINee's KEY, Kim Ki Bum's donation speech)

713
00:26:43,569 --> 00:26:48,300
Actually, my first donation was to this hospital,

714
00:26:48,300 --> 00:26:49,339
and...

715
00:26:49,710 --> 00:26:52,409
my dad said that I should donate some more money...

716
00:26:52,409 --> 00:26:54,010
if I wanted to boost my mom's ego.

717
00:26:54,010 --> 00:26:55,280
(Joking)

718
00:26:55,649 --> 00:26:58,149
- That's right. - I tried to lighten the mood.

719
00:26:58,149 --> 00:26:59,250
- Good job. - Nice.

720
00:26:59,250 --> 00:27:03,450
I just wanted this money to be used for more people in need.

721
00:27:03,450 --> 00:27:06,460
And I will also be rooting for...

722
00:27:06,720 --> 00:27:08,220
everyone who will regain their health.

723
00:27:08,220 --> 00:27:09,690
- Thank you. - Yes, thank you.

724
00:27:09,790 --> 00:27:12,230
(Thank you.)

725
00:27:12,760 --> 00:27:15,000
Next, we will have the donation delivery ceremony.

726
00:27:15,000 --> 00:27:16,230
Have you not given it to them yet?

727
00:27:16,230 --> 00:27:17,700
Please let him go home.

728
00:27:18,369 --> 00:27:19,700
You should come up too. Let's take a picture.

729
00:27:19,700 --> 00:27:20,869
Right, Mom. Come on up.

730
00:27:21,639 --> 00:27:23,139
- You have to do this. - Yes, you do.

731
00:27:23,409 --> 00:27:24,639
("SHINee's KEY, Donates 50,000 Dollars...")

732
00:27:24,639 --> 00:27:25,839
("to Children's Hospital Patients")

733
00:27:25,839 --> 00:27:28,309
This was almost as grand as an awards ceremony.

734
00:27:28,950 --> 00:27:30,780
It's like you already went to the Entertainment Awards.

735
00:27:30,780 --> 00:27:32,180
(By yourself)

736
00:27:32,780 --> 00:27:34,680
With this, we will conclude today's ceremony.

737
00:27:34,680 --> 00:27:35,849
- Goodness. - Thank you.

738
00:27:36,490 --> 00:27:37,649
"Meeting adjourned."

739
00:27:38,720 --> 00:27:39,720
Thank you.

740
00:27:39,720 --> 00:27:41,790
- You look a lot like your mother. - Right?

741
00:27:42,460 --> 00:27:43,530
Then, here...

742
00:27:44,930 --> 00:27:46,899
(This isn't an easy thing to do.)

743
00:27:47,530 --> 00:27:49,770
I was so drained. I look gaunt.

744
00:27:50,700 --> 00:27:53,369
Every time I go to the hospital, my mom never forgets...

745
00:27:53,540 --> 00:27:56,609
to take me to meet the young patients battling cancer.

746
00:27:56,809 --> 00:27:57,809
Oh, to say hi.

747
00:27:57,809 --> 00:27:59,980
So, we made rounds on two more floors and came back up.

748
00:27:59,980 --> 00:28:01,210
Gosh, you did so well.

749
00:28:01,639 --> 00:28:04,050
- We're back. - Hello.

750
00:28:04,280 --> 00:28:06,720
- Hang on. I have to... - She's going home now, right?

751
00:28:06,720 --> 00:28:08,649
- Yes. - Do you need to take this too?

752
00:28:10,190 --> 00:28:11,720
My books and...

753
00:28:11,720 --> 00:28:12,960
(It's time to clean out her beloved desk too.)

754
00:28:14,690 --> 00:28:16,190
- All her things... - I have these too.

755
00:28:16,190 --> 00:28:17,629
and the presents that she got.

756
00:28:18,159 --> 00:28:20,730
Nurse Kim, have you signed everything that needs approval?

757
00:28:21,329 --> 00:28:23,800
I have signed everything that I got today.

758
00:28:26,839 --> 00:28:29,010
If I missed something, I'll do it once I get home.

759
00:28:30,639 --> 00:28:32,579
Gosh, she's leaving for good now.

760
00:28:33,180 --> 00:28:35,079
How would she feel to clear everything out?

761
00:28:35,079 --> 00:28:36,149
Oh, goodness.

762
00:28:36,149 --> 00:28:37,409
(The end of a great chapter of 36 years and 8 months)

763
00:28:38,349 --> 00:28:39,819
My photo from when I received an award.

764
00:28:39,819 --> 00:28:40,980
(Chuckling)

765
00:28:41,649 --> 00:28:44,389
(Her regular daily life spent here...)

766
00:28:44,389 --> 00:28:46,690
- Will you be okay? - Yes, I'll put it in like this.

767
00:28:46,889 --> 00:28:48,829
The day for clearing out Mom's space has actually arrived.

768
00:28:49,030 --> 00:28:50,530
That's what I kept thinking.

769
00:28:50,690 --> 00:28:53,260
It was like when I graduated...

770
00:28:53,260 --> 00:28:54,960
and cleared out my desk thinking,

771
00:28:54,960 --> 00:28:56,669
"Am I really not coming back to this school?"

772
00:28:57,200 --> 00:28:59,800
I can't even imagine what that feels like.

773
00:29:00,270 --> 00:29:01,470
- Let's go. - Let's go.

774
00:29:01,470 --> 00:29:02,970
- I'll be going now. - Bye.

775
00:29:02,970 --> 00:29:05,440
Thank you for everything.

776
00:29:05,440 --> 00:29:07,109
- Bye. - Bye.

777
00:29:07,109 --> 00:29:08,510
I'll visit you at home later.

778
00:29:08,510 --> 00:29:10,109
- Okay. - This feels so weird.

779
00:29:10,750 --> 00:29:12,119
The last time riding the elevator.

780
00:29:12,119 --> 00:29:14,550
(It's the last time riding the elevator she took every day.)

781
00:29:19,520 --> 00:29:20,990
- Let's go. - Okay.

782
00:29:20,990 --> 00:29:23,159
- Come on. - Let's go.

783
00:29:23,960 --> 00:29:25,030
I'll drive.

784
00:29:25,030 --> 00:29:26,399
- Will you drive? - Yes, give me the key.

785
00:29:27,599 --> 00:29:31,569
(She's with her son on the last trip home from work.)

786
00:29:32,040 --> 00:29:33,300
Let's go.

787
00:29:34,040 --> 00:29:36,339
Go straight.

788
00:29:36,710 --> 00:29:39,040
Yes, and move over to the first lane now.

789
00:29:39,040 --> 00:29:41,139
She must've driven on this road so many times.

790
00:29:43,550 --> 00:29:44,680
Goodness, now...

791
00:29:46,419 --> 00:29:48,619
I won't be coming here very often anymore.

792
00:29:48,950 --> 00:29:50,149
- She must be kind of sad. - Yes.

793
00:29:50,149 --> 00:29:51,319
It doesn't feel real yet.

794
00:29:51,419 --> 00:29:53,059
- I can't even imagine how it feels. - Right.

795
00:29:54,089 --> 00:29:56,859
I mean, at the hospital, I have work to do.

796
00:29:56,859 --> 00:29:58,260
So, it didn't feel real.

797
00:29:58,530 --> 00:30:02,430
Even until today, I had work to do.

798
00:30:02,970 --> 00:30:06,069
There wasn't much time to think about things.

799
00:30:07,300 --> 00:30:08,669
Earlier at the hospital,

800
00:30:08,669 --> 00:30:11,210
- things were so hectic that... - Right, at the hospital...

801
00:30:11,210 --> 00:30:13,079
- it didn't even feel real. - Right.

802
00:30:13,079 --> 00:30:14,480
- Yes. - I'm sure it didn't.

803
00:30:14,879 --> 00:30:18,280
Goodness, I get to go home from work for the last time...

804
00:30:18,680 --> 00:30:20,680
with my son.

805
00:30:21,450 --> 00:30:22,889
It's so touching.

806
00:30:23,290 --> 00:30:25,050
- It's weird. - I know.

807
00:30:25,419 --> 00:30:26,960
You were so busy,

808
00:30:27,290 --> 00:30:29,730
but you made time for me for a day at the end of the year.

809
00:30:30,190 --> 00:30:31,730
I'm so grateful.

810
00:30:31,730 --> 00:30:33,829
- Gosh, you did so well. - You really did.

811
00:30:33,829 --> 00:30:35,000
- You must be a reliable son to her. - So nice.

812
00:30:35,000 --> 00:30:37,300
How many people get to do this?

813
00:30:37,300 --> 00:30:38,300
(She's even prouder that her son is driving for her.)

814
00:30:38,700 --> 00:30:41,800
Before I started driving, Dad came to pick me up every day.

815
00:30:41,800 --> 00:30:43,339
- That's amazing. - And he'd drop me off.

816
00:30:43,540 --> 00:30:44,710
Was it 9 o'clock when we left?

817
00:30:44,710 --> 00:30:46,909
I remember going there in the morning.

818
00:30:46,909 --> 00:30:48,510
- Oh, really? - Yes.

819
00:30:48,510 --> 00:30:50,909
- When we went to pick you up, - Yes?

820
00:30:50,909 --> 00:30:52,450
I used to sleep in the car and stuff.

821
00:30:52,450 --> 00:30:53,450
(The boy in the back seat has grown up in no time...)

822
00:30:53,450 --> 00:30:55,149
(to drive his mom home now.)

823
00:30:55,819 --> 00:30:59,589
When the sun sets like this, the drive home is so nice.

824
00:30:59,589 --> 00:31:00,589
(Right then, the orange sky also congratulates her last trip home.)

825
00:31:01,889 --> 00:31:03,629
Starting January 2,

826
00:31:05,530 --> 00:31:07,260
what do I do if I don't go to work?

827
00:31:08,000 --> 00:31:09,700
It feels like that in the morning.

828
00:31:09,869 --> 00:31:12,639
- That's right. - When it's time to go to work.

829
00:31:16,369 --> 00:31:18,639
These days,

830
00:31:20,510 --> 00:31:22,649
I keep thinking about your grandma.

831
00:31:23,109 --> 00:31:24,909
- Really? - Yes.

832
00:31:25,379 --> 00:31:28,180
Your grandma was a strong woman, you know?

833
00:31:28,280 --> 00:31:30,050
She was ahead of her time.

834
00:31:30,619 --> 00:31:33,990
When I was growing up, your grandma...

835
00:31:34,619 --> 00:31:36,990
Back then, people didn't send daughters...

836
00:31:36,990 --> 00:31:39,030
- to college that often. - They didn't.

837
00:31:39,030 --> 00:31:40,899
- It was like that back then. - Right.

838
00:31:40,899 --> 00:31:43,899
But your grandma said she would support our education...

839
00:31:43,899 --> 00:31:46,440
as long as we studied hard, regardless of gender.

840
00:31:46,440 --> 00:31:47,599
That's amazing.

841
00:31:47,940 --> 00:31:50,770
She said that even women had to work.

842
00:31:51,270 --> 00:31:54,139
She said that a woman had to have financial independence...

843
00:31:54,280 --> 00:31:56,879
to be able to have her own opinions and live her own life.

844
00:31:57,879 --> 00:31:59,579
- She was a forward thinker. - Yes, she was.

845
00:31:59,579 --> 00:32:01,379
- She was very progressive. - She really was.

846
00:32:01,379 --> 00:32:05,250
My aunt became a doctor back then too.

847
00:32:06,190 --> 00:32:07,690
I think that's so cool.

848
00:32:08,659 --> 00:32:10,159
I'm just so grateful.

849
00:32:10,159 --> 00:32:11,559
This was your first job.

850
00:32:11,559 --> 00:32:14,159
- Gosh, she stayed at her 1st job. - For 36 years.

851
00:32:14,730 --> 00:32:16,430
It's so impressive that our elders did that.

852
00:32:17,399 --> 00:32:19,569
Because I was working,

853
00:32:19,839 --> 00:32:21,899
I couldn't take care of you. Weren't you sad about anything?

854
00:32:22,809 --> 00:32:24,109
Well, I just...

855
00:32:26,379 --> 00:32:30,109
When I was little, I just thought all parents were like that.

856
00:32:32,119 --> 00:32:33,349
That was my daily life.

857
00:32:33,349 --> 00:32:35,319
When I got home, Grandma would be there.

858
00:32:35,720 --> 00:32:36,819
I didn't know anything else.

859
00:32:36,950 --> 00:32:39,220
I don't feel lonely about not having siblings either.

860
00:32:39,220 --> 00:32:40,490
I've never had them, so I wouldn't know.

861
00:32:41,220 --> 00:32:42,790
I've always been an only child.

862
00:32:42,790 --> 00:32:44,389
(Her only son grew up bravely on his own.)

863
00:32:44,490 --> 00:32:46,800
Until I turned ten years old,

864
00:32:46,800 --> 00:32:49,030
I remember that Mom always worked in the emergency room.

865
00:32:49,230 --> 00:32:51,169
I would always go in front of the ER...

866
00:32:51,169 --> 00:32:52,339
and tell my mom...

867
00:32:52,339 --> 00:32:53,970
that she did a great job today.

868
00:32:54,200 --> 00:32:56,069
That was a big part of my life.

869
00:32:56,309 --> 00:32:58,470
I've seen her since she worked a three-shift schedule.

870
00:32:58,940 --> 00:33:00,309
Whenever she came home,

871
00:33:00,309 --> 00:33:03,909
I remember Mom looking so tired.

872
00:33:03,909 --> 00:33:05,780
Now that I look back,

873
00:33:06,280 --> 00:33:08,889
she was married, raising a child,

874
00:33:09,220 --> 00:33:11,220
and working the night shift.

875
00:33:11,220 --> 00:33:12,790
- I learned what that meant later. - Gosh, that makes no sense.

876
00:33:12,889 --> 00:33:14,419
- Just one of those would be hard. - It makes no sense.

877
00:33:15,059 --> 00:33:16,829
We haven't done any of those things yet.

878
00:33:17,059 --> 00:33:21,059
I've never seen her rest on both days of the weekend.

879
00:33:21,430 --> 00:33:24,230
But with that day of rest, we'd go on family trips to Mount Jiri.

880
00:33:25,500 --> 00:33:26,970
She'd work six days a week,

881
00:33:26,970 --> 00:33:29,970
and on her one day off, she'd take her kid out to play.

882
00:33:29,970 --> 00:33:31,770
It really makes no sense at all.

883
00:33:32,040 --> 00:33:34,510
All I can say is that she's amazing.

884
00:33:34,510 --> 00:33:36,280
If I were her, could I have endured it?

885
00:33:36,280 --> 00:33:38,349
I sometimes think about that too.

886
00:33:38,649 --> 00:33:40,280
Because it's been 36 years.

887
00:33:40,819 --> 00:33:42,250
Every single day.

888
00:33:42,250 --> 00:33:44,649
Maybe, she worked so sincerely...

889
00:33:44,649 --> 00:33:47,490
because she wanted to be a better mom for me.

890
00:33:47,490 --> 00:33:51,030
I think that could've been part of it.

891
00:33:51,559 --> 00:33:54,329
I liked that I was there for the last part of her journey.

892
00:33:55,399 --> 00:33:57,329
She must've been so happy.

893
00:33:57,329 --> 00:33:58,829
(He's a reliable presence for his mom's last trip home.)

894
00:33:58,829 --> 00:34:00,040
Let's go inside.

895
00:34:03,240 --> 00:34:04,240
(They return home with their hands full of stuff.)

896
00:34:04,240 --> 00:34:05,339
Oh, it's dark.

897
00:34:05,339 --> 00:34:07,309
(They return home with their hands full of stuff.)

898
00:34:08,379 --> 00:34:09,609
Gosh, let me go in first.

899
00:34:10,750 --> 00:34:12,280
- Okay. - I have to go in first.

900
00:34:12,419 --> 00:34:13,619
- What? - I have to go in first.

901
00:34:13,619 --> 00:34:15,180
- I had set it up. - Oh, gosh.

902
00:34:16,149 --> 00:34:18,890
(The surprise party that KEY had prepared since morning)

903
00:34:18,890 --> 00:34:20,689
You were sweating buckets as you prepared it.

904
00:34:21,959 --> 00:34:24,390
- You'll see it when it gets bright. - Yes.

905
00:34:25,729 --> 00:34:28,100
- Ta-da! - What's this?

906
00:34:28,700 --> 00:34:30,970
- Ta-da! - What's this?

907
00:34:30,970 --> 00:34:32,240
(She jumps up as if she didn't expect it.)

908
00:34:32,240 --> 00:34:33,300
She jumped up.

909
00:34:34,269 --> 00:34:35,370
What's this?

910
00:34:35,370 --> 00:34:36,410
Why?

911
00:34:36,669 --> 00:34:37,740
What's this?

912
00:34:38,339 --> 00:34:40,479
- Come on! - Oh, no!

913
00:34:40,709 --> 00:34:42,350
- You didn't stick it on firmly. - I should've checked.

914
00:34:42,350 --> 00:34:44,310
- The house must've been too hot. - It was really...

915
00:34:44,709 --> 00:34:46,080
Oh, my. What is this?

916
00:34:46,080 --> 00:34:47,080
(So touched)

917
00:34:47,080 --> 00:34:48,379
Seriously, what is this?

918
00:34:48,379 --> 00:34:49,390
(Flustered)

919
00:34:50,720 --> 00:34:52,859
- Did you do this? - What is this? I didn't...

920
00:34:53,459 --> 00:34:54,560
Darn it.

921
00:34:55,089 --> 00:34:56,660
- Why? Did it fall off? - Hang on. Yes.

922
00:34:57,260 --> 00:34:58,390
Darn it. What's this?

923
00:34:59,399 --> 00:35:00,399
Darn it.

924
00:35:00,399 --> 00:35:02,470
- Did you do this before you left? - Yes.

925
00:35:02,470 --> 00:35:04,300
I set the whole thing up earlier.

926
00:35:04,569 --> 00:35:06,300
No, but your mom liked it.

927
00:35:06,669 --> 00:35:07,839
She looks so happy.

928
00:35:07,839 --> 00:35:09,410
My mom just liked that.

929
00:35:10,010 --> 00:35:11,169
- My goodness. - Here, look at this.

930
00:35:11,439 --> 00:35:12,709
(Picking it up)

931
00:35:12,910 --> 00:35:14,410
- My goodness. - Here, look at this.

932
00:35:15,280 --> 00:35:16,379
What is it?

933
00:35:16,379 --> 00:35:17,450
(And he picks up...)

934
00:35:17,450 --> 00:35:19,280
- Oh, my! - Goodness.

935
00:35:19,479 --> 00:35:20,519
My gosh.

936
00:35:20,519 --> 00:35:21,819
(A photo of his mom when she was younger)

937
00:35:23,550 --> 00:35:26,260
(Surprised and touched, How did you prepare all this?)

938
00:35:26,260 --> 00:35:27,359
Isn't it funny?

939
00:35:27,359 --> 00:35:30,189
Goodness, what is this?

940
00:35:34,229 --> 00:35:36,129
It was when I was in my third year at college,

941
00:35:36,129 --> 00:35:37,269
for my white hat ceremony.

942
00:35:37,269 --> 00:35:38,300
A white hat ceremony?

943
00:35:38,300 --> 00:35:40,870
It's the day nursing students get their nurse's hats.

944
00:35:41,569 --> 00:35:42,609
- These days, - It's like...

945
00:35:42,609 --> 00:35:43,640
they call it the Nightingale Pledge.

946
00:35:44,439 --> 00:35:46,680
- They look like fairies. - My gosh.

947
00:35:47,839 --> 00:35:49,109
This must've been when Mom was in college.

948
00:35:49,109 --> 00:35:50,910
(KEY was looking through old albums in the morning.)

949
00:35:50,910 --> 00:35:52,050
The white hat ceremony.

950
00:35:52,050 --> 00:35:53,819
(The white hat ceremony...)

951
00:35:54,080 --> 00:35:55,580
I remembered that I had seen...

952
00:35:55,580 --> 00:35:58,589
photos of my mom at her white hat ceremony.

953
00:35:58,589 --> 00:35:59,760
The nurses... Oh, here it is.

954
00:35:59,760 --> 00:36:01,189
- You took that out. - Yes.

955
00:36:01,189 --> 00:36:02,830
At the end of this chapter in her life,

956
00:36:02,830 --> 00:36:05,629
I wanted to congratulate her with the photo of her beginning.

957
00:36:05,629 --> 00:36:07,859
I wanted to deliver a message that she did so well.

958
00:36:07,859 --> 00:36:09,330
So, I wrote a letter on the back of the photo too.

959
00:36:09,930 --> 00:36:11,530
- You're like an event planner. - That's so cool.

960
00:36:11,530 --> 00:36:12,700
You did so well.

961
00:36:13,370 --> 00:36:16,310
Goodness, what is this?

962
00:36:17,370 --> 00:36:18,609
- Really? - Yes.

963
00:36:19,740 --> 00:36:21,039
My son, come here.

964
00:36:21,740 --> 00:36:23,550
All the children in this country hate this video.

965
00:36:23,709 --> 00:36:25,149
Where else would I find a son like this?

966
00:36:25,149 --> 00:36:26,879
Seriously, Ki Bum is the only one.

967
00:36:27,050 --> 00:36:30,120
All the other children in the country will get cursed out.

968
00:36:30,850 --> 00:36:32,319
Gosh, this is making me cry.

969
00:36:32,319 --> 00:36:34,089
- Why are you crying again? - Oh, goodness.

970
00:36:34,319 --> 00:36:36,959
It's not an easy thing for a son to do this for his mom.

971
00:36:38,729 --> 00:36:40,060
Goodness, thank you.

972
00:36:42,600 --> 00:36:44,800
- Start taking photos. - Photos?

973
00:36:44,800 --> 00:36:45,939
I'll cook for you.

974
00:36:46,039 --> 00:36:47,269
- Gosh, that's cool. - Little punk.

975
00:36:47,269 --> 00:36:48,539
"I'll cook for you." My goodness.

976
00:36:48,539 --> 00:36:49,669
That was so cool just now.

977
00:36:51,109 --> 00:36:53,410
Your mom's group chat will be blowing up now.

978
00:36:53,709 --> 00:36:54,879
Group chat.

979
00:36:55,209 --> 00:36:56,510
My goodness.

980
00:36:57,510 --> 00:36:59,220
(She's full of smiles at the thought of bragging about her son.)

981
00:36:59,220 --> 00:37:01,280
She looks so happy.

982
00:37:02,149 --> 00:37:03,749
(Grabbing)

983
00:37:04,789 --> 00:37:07,990
- Is it okay I wrote on the photo? - Yes.

984
00:37:07,990 --> 00:37:08,990
(She reads the sincere letter thoroughly as well.)

985
00:37:09,189 --> 00:37:10,560
(To my incredible mom!)

986
00:37:10,560 --> 00:37:13,300
I still have so many memories from when my mom...

987
00:37:13,300 --> 00:37:15,260
worked in tougher conditions.

988
00:37:15,260 --> 00:37:17,229
I can't believe that she's already retiring.

989
00:37:17,229 --> 00:37:18,800
She worked really hard.

990
00:37:18,800 --> 00:37:20,640
I think that's what I wanted to express on that photo.

991
00:37:20,640 --> 00:37:21,769
That was the beginning.

992
00:37:22,939 --> 00:37:25,109
I will treasure this for my whole life.

993
00:37:25,109 --> 00:37:28,879
(I will treasure this for my whole life.)

994
00:37:30,479 --> 00:37:31,950
Gosh, thank you.

995
00:37:32,450 --> 00:37:34,919
Don't mention it. You can sit and wait.

996
00:37:35,890 --> 00:37:37,390
Let me go and get changed.

997
00:37:39,050 --> 00:37:42,089
(While his mom is changing, KEY gets dinner ready.)

998
00:37:42,089 --> 00:37:43,789
There's no other son like you.

999
00:37:44,030 --> 00:37:45,660
I feel so bad for my mom.

1000
00:37:48,030 --> 00:37:50,700
(He checks the food one last time too.)

1001
00:37:51,769 --> 00:37:53,240
Do you know what this is?

1002
00:37:53,700 --> 00:37:54,769
What is it?

1003
00:37:55,240 --> 00:37:57,269
This is Grandma's braised crab.

1004
00:37:57,810 --> 00:37:58,970
Really?

1005
00:37:58,970 --> 00:38:00,010
(Giggling)

1006
00:38:01,640 --> 00:38:04,149
Goodness, how did you think to make this?

1007
00:38:04,609 --> 00:38:06,850
I wanted to eat it.

1008
00:38:06,850 --> 00:38:08,080
- Really? - Yes.

1009
00:38:09,050 --> 00:38:11,789
Ki Bum, you know exactly what your mom would like.

1010
00:38:13,319 --> 00:38:14,819
Gosh, this is big. Look.

1011
00:38:16,129 --> 00:38:17,129
(A whole, plump crab)

1012
00:38:17,129 --> 00:38:18,890
- That looks so good. - We should eat one each.

1013
00:38:18,890 --> 00:38:20,660
- Goodness. - I should eat some crab too.

1014
00:38:20,660 --> 00:38:21,729
(Expectant)

1015
00:38:21,729 --> 00:38:23,769
Did you make this because you remembered...

1016
00:38:23,769 --> 00:38:25,399
- Grandma's braised crab? - Yes.

1017
00:38:25,430 --> 00:38:26,569
I wanted it so bad,

1018
00:38:26,569 --> 00:38:28,470
- but they don't sell it anywhere. - I know.

1019
00:38:28,470 --> 00:38:31,109
I figured out the recipe after several attempts.

1020
00:38:31,109 --> 00:38:32,810
I tried cooking it about three times before.

1021
00:38:32,910 --> 00:38:34,180
- Oh, really? - Yes.

1022
00:38:34,410 --> 00:38:37,450
Since you're good at cooking, you can recall those old memories.

1023
00:38:38,280 --> 00:38:40,379
- I'll enjoy the meal. - I'll enjoy the meal.

1024
00:38:40,950 --> 00:38:42,649
- It's hot. - How should I cut it?

1025
00:38:43,350 --> 00:38:45,490
- Take the back... - Yes?

1026
00:38:45,490 --> 00:38:48,620
and hold it. Then you flip this part over.

1027
00:38:48,819 --> 00:38:51,060
- This back part? - Yes. That's it.

1028
00:38:51,060 --> 00:38:52,189
(She opens it as KEY demonstrated.)

1029
00:38:52,189 --> 00:38:53,359
- Like this? - Yes, like that.

1030
00:38:54,100 --> 00:38:55,359
- Gosh, this is... - The crabs have eggs in them now.

1031
00:38:55,499 --> 00:38:57,530
- The soy sauce is all absorbed. - Yes.

1032
00:38:57,999 --> 00:38:59,069
Oh, my gosh.

1033
00:38:59,499 --> 00:39:00,640
What is all this?

1034
00:39:00,640 --> 00:39:03,010
I never even thought to make something like this.

1035
00:39:03,709 --> 00:39:05,669
I'm so curious. Will it taste the same?

1036
00:39:06,109 --> 00:39:08,740
It tasted similar to me, but it should taste like that to you.

1037
00:39:09,709 --> 00:39:11,580
Let me see if it's seasoned right first.

1038
00:39:14,550 --> 00:39:16,419
- How is it? Does it taste the same? - It tastes just like Grandma's.

1039
00:39:16,419 --> 00:39:18,220
- Really? - I want to try that.

1040
00:39:19,120 --> 00:39:20,760
(Delighted)

1041
00:39:20,760 --> 00:39:22,459
- Gosh. - I can't imagine how it tastes.

1042
00:39:22,660 --> 00:39:24,830
- It's something we've never tried. - Right.

1043
00:39:24,830 --> 00:39:25,830
(A new dish that makes you curious of how it tastes)

1044
00:39:25,859 --> 00:39:27,959
- I'll get some on the rice. - Right.

1045
00:39:27,959 --> 00:39:29,430
(Placing the seasoned innards and roe on top of the rice)

1046
00:39:30,430 --> 00:39:32,600
(Taking a bite of the rich taste of the crab)

1047
00:39:32,999 --> 00:39:34,069
It's good, isn't it?

1048
00:39:34,700 --> 00:39:35,970
(The son grabs it with his hand...)

1049
00:39:35,970 --> 00:39:37,769
(and tears off the meat.)

1050
00:39:37,769 --> 00:39:40,310
I've never thought about making this.

1051
00:39:40,680 --> 00:39:41,839
- Even when I wanted to eat this. - That's the face you make...

1052
00:39:41,839 --> 00:39:42,910
when it's a familiar taste.

1053
00:39:42,979 --> 00:39:45,580
It's so good. Even though I made it, it's good.

1054
00:39:45,649 --> 00:39:47,149
Teach me the recipe.

1055
00:39:49,319 --> 00:39:50,990
I'll try making it too.

1056
00:39:51,350 --> 00:39:55,359
I brought the seed soy sauce from home.

1057
00:39:55,359 --> 00:39:56,689
- You did? - I used that for the sauce.

1058
00:39:56,789 --> 00:40:00,859
You can have this with Dad when I'm not around.

1059
00:40:01,129 --> 00:40:02,629
I took out all of the ingredients.

1060
00:40:02,830 --> 00:40:04,069
Use just a little bit of them.

1061
00:40:04,069 --> 00:40:06,069
Take a picture of them and use a little bit of them.

1062
00:40:06,069 --> 00:40:07,539
I placed all of them together.

1063
00:40:07,870 --> 00:40:10,069
When you try just the soy sauce,

1064
00:40:10,370 --> 00:40:11,470
you have to go, "It's salty."

1065
00:40:11,470 --> 00:40:14,539
That way, it will taste good with the crab.

1066
00:40:14,539 --> 00:40:15,580
- As it gets into the meat. - Right.

1067
00:40:15,580 --> 00:40:17,910
- But this is so good. - It's good, isn't it?

1068
00:40:18,050 --> 00:40:19,680
I'm having this after 20 years.

1069
00:40:19,680 --> 00:40:21,180
- It must be the same for you. - Right.

1070
00:40:21,280 --> 00:40:22,749
There's no place that makes it like this.

1071
00:40:23,890 --> 00:40:24,890
(The braised crab in soy sauce has returned thanks to her son.)

1072
00:40:24,890 --> 00:40:26,589
- It must be really good. - Right.

1073
00:40:26,819 --> 00:40:29,089
My mom usually never eats with her hands.

1074
00:40:29,990 --> 00:40:31,089
She's really enjoying it.

1075
00:40:31,089 --> 00:40:32,089
(Entranced)

1076
00:40:32,660 --> 00:40:34,760
(Mom is really enjoying it.)

1077
00:40:34,830 --> 00:40:36,569
Aren't I eating too well?

1078
00:40:37,470 --> 00:40:39,870
- Gosh. - It's fascinating to see this.

1079
00:40:41,399 --> 00:40:43,870
- I've never imagined this. - Gosh.

1080
00:40:43,870 --> 00:40:45,039
It's so good.

1081
00:40:45,039 --> 00:40:46,339
Crabs are dangerous.

1082
00:40:47,240 --> 00:40:50,010
Cooking is fine,

1083
00:40:50,010 --> 00:40:53,050
but it's the rest that feels tiresome.

1084
00:40:54,580 --> 00:40:57,919
(Taking a bite after getting a big spoonful)

1085
00:40:58,789 --> 00:41:02,220
(Eating without stopping)

1086
00:41:02,519 --> 00:41:03,789
- I'm so curious. - I'm so curious...

1087
00:41:03,789 --> 00:41:04,890
about how it tastes.

1088
00:41:05,660 --> 00:41:07,760
It looks like braised chicken.

1089
00:41:07,760 --> 00:41:09,870
- It does look like jjimdak. - Right.

1090
00:41:10,069 --> 00:41:12,740
There's the braised chicken and crab stew these days.

1091
00:41:12,939 --> 00:41:14,700
- That dish is red. - Right.

1092
00:41:14,800 --> 00:41:16,910
Jjimdak is made just with soy sauce.

1093
00:41:17,169 --> 00:41:19,010
It's right in between those two dishes.

1094
00:41:19,439 --> 00:41:22,339
I added in some Cheongyang red peppers.

1095
00:41:22,580 --> 00:41:24,010
It's spicy...

1096
00:41:24,010 --> 00:41:26,419
- and sweet with the soy sauce. - Gosh, that must be great.

1097
00:41:26,850 --> 00:41:28,680
- Dad would really like this. - Right.

1098
00:41:28,680 --> 00:41:30,649
My dad came that night.

1099
00:41:30,649 --> 00:41:31,720
Really?

1100
00:41:32,589 --> 00:41:34,789
- She emptied a whole bowl. - She ate more than I did.

1101
00:41:34,789 --> 00:41:36,530
You're eating really well.

1102
00:41:37,859 --> 00:41:39,560
What's going on today?

1103
00:41:40,359 --> 00:41:43,700
My goodness. Ms. Kim Sun Hee emptied a whole bowl of rice.

1104
00:41:44,499 --> 00:41:46,970
(Clean)

1105
00:41:47,339 --> 00:41:49,300
- I have something to give you too. - What?

1106
00:41:49,439 --> 00:41:52,010
I have no reason to get anything.

1107
00:41:52,140 --> 00:41:53,979
Wait right there. I prepared something for you too.

1108
00:41:54,709 --> 00:41:56,249
- What? - What could it be?

1109
00:41:56,249 --> 00:41:57,379
What could it be?

1110
00:41:57,649 --> 00:41:59,749
(His mother brings out something from her room.)

1111
00:42:00,479 --> 00:42:03,149
- We must be quite similar. - What is it?

1112
00:42:04,249 --> 00:42:05,419
A letter?

1113
00:42:06,189 --> 00:42:07,959
- It's a letter. - What is it?

1114
00:42:08,789 --> 00:42:11,589
Is it a Christmas card from you?

1115
00:42:12,490 --> 00:42:13,700
It's so pretty!

1116
00:42:13,700 --> 00:42:15,499
We always give each other these things.

1117
00:42:15,499 --> 00:42:16,600
- Really? - Yes.

1118
00:42:16,600 --> 00:42:17,700
That's so nice.

1119
00:42:17,899 --> 00:42:19,269
It was so pretty.

1120
00:42:19,269 --> 00:42:20,269
(I wanted to get it for you as it was so pretty.)

1121
00:42:20,769 --> 00:42:22,039
(Along with the small handwritten letter...)

1122
00:42:22,039 --> 00:42:24,039
- This is separate. - Right.

1123
00:42:24,609 --> 00:42:25,609
(Gasping)

1124
00:42:26,609 --> 00:42:27,709
Money?

1125
00:42:27,709 --> 00:42:28,709
Gosh.

1126
00:42:29,950 --> 00:42:31,280
Why all of a sudden?

1127
00:42:31,680 --> 00:42:33,720
Every Christmas,

1128
00:42:33,720 --> 00:42:35,550
- Really? - you gave me money.

1129
00:42:36,249 --> 00:42:38,050
I should make my mom watch this part only.

1130
00:42:38,050 --> 00:42:39,089
Me too. Just this part.

1131
00:42:39,089 --> 00:42:40,419
- Just send this moment. - Right.

1132
00:42:40,419 --> 00:42:41,660
- This is the perfect moment. - Right.

1133
00:42:42,519 --> 00:42:45,490
I think this will cover up for everything I did.

1134
00:42:46,200 --> 00:42:48,260
I had this thought in the past.

1135
00:42:48,859 --> 00:42:50,669
I had to take care of my child as he was my child.

1136
00:42:50,669 --> 00:42:53,269
I had to take care of my parents as they were my parents.

1137
00:42:53,569 --> 00:42:55,169
"What about me?"

1138
00:42:56,240 --> 00:42:58,069
It's the same for you right now.

1139
00:42:58,240 --> 00:43:01,010
There are times when you want to be taken care of...

1140
00:43:01,010 --> 00:43:02,240
- from someone. - Of course.

1141
00:43:02,680 --> 00:43:04,550
I wanted to do that for you.

1142
00:43:05,080 --> 00:43:07,019
You went through so much throughout the year.

1143
00:43:07,019 --> 00:43:08,879
You did too.

1144
00:43:09,589 --> 00:43:10,990
What's wrong with your ear? Is it bleeding?

1145
00:43:10,990 --> 00:43:12,450
- No. I was sweating. - He felt hot.

1146
00:43:12,450 --> 00:43:14,689
What's the temperature inside the house?

1147
00:43:14,689 --> 00:43:16,330
- Gosh. - Is it a sauna?

1148
00:43:16,330 --> 00:43:18,660
Please stop my parents.

1149
00:43:19,060 --> 00:43:20,330
Do you know the feeling...

1150
00:43:20,330 --> 00:43:21,760
- of the house boiling? - I do.

1151
00:43:22,060 --> 00:43:23,800
- It's because you're a singer. - Right.

1152
00:43:24,269 --> 00:43:25,870
They're always worried that I would feel cold.

1153
00:43:25,870 --> 00:43:26,899
(Her mother wanted to keep him warm since the morning.)

1154
00:43:26,899 --> 00:43:27,939
Gosh.

1155
00:43:28,399 --> 00:43:31,569
It still feels unreal to me,

1156
00:43:32,209 --> 00:43:34,039
but I wanted to make it a grand party.

1157
00:43:34,839 --> 00:43:37,709
I didn't expect this at all.

1158
00:43:37,879 --> 00:43:39,879
(We root for the 2nd chapter of your shining life.)

1159
00:43:39,879 --> 00:43:43,950
My mom's life that I see...

1160
00:43:44,689 --> 00:43:49,419
seems to have a lot of interesting moments in her future.

1161
00:43:49,729 --> 00:43:51,859
There were things that she wasn't able to do...

1162
00:43:51,859 --> 00:43:53,560
because of me or the family.

1163
00:43:53,560 --> 00:43:54,930
I hope that she will do...

1164
00:43:55,800 --> 00:43:57,669
everything she wishes to now.

1165
00:43:58,100 --> 00:44:01,039
I really wish for her to live freely now.

1166
00:44:01,439 --> 00:44:03,669
"The way you worked..."

1167
00:44:03,839 --> 00:44:06,539
"really affected the way I worked."

1168
00:44:06,810 --> 00:44:08,780
She can study whatever she wants to study.

1169
00:44:08,780 --> 00:44:10,979
She can enjoy her hobbies and work out.

1170
00:44:11,109 --> 00:44:14,080
If there's something that she needs, I will definitely help out.

1171
00:44:15,280 --> 00:44:17,649
I think it's my duty to help her out well.

1172
00:44:17,649 --> 00:44:18,819
He's so reliable.

1173
00:44:19,319 --> 00:44:20,620
Thank you.

1174
00:44:21,589 --> 00:44:23,760
We should celebrate one more time when Dad comes.

1175
00:44:24,129 --> 00:44:27,129
We ate that together that night.

1176
00:44:27,499 --> 00:44:29,060
I'll stay the night and leave tomorrow.

1177
00:44:29,060 --> 00:44:30,229
Sure.

1178
00:44:30,229 --> 00:44:31,800
You can go after seeing your dad.

1179
00:44:31,970 --> 00:44:34,700
Sleeping over for two nights means you're the best son.

1180
00:44:34,970 --> 00:44:37,740
I would have ten sons if they were like Ki Bum.

1181
00:44:39,539 --> 00:44:42,850
(Walking Mom for Act 2 of Her Life)

1182
00:44:42,850 --> 00:44:43,850
(Home Alone)

1183
00:44:43,850 --> 00:44:46,220
After Kwak Do Gyu,

1184
00:44:46,220 --> 00:44:48,850
we have a new smart Rainbow Club member...

1185
00:44:48,850 --> 00:44:50,490
- waiting for us. - Great.

1186
00:44:50,550 --> 00:44:52,649
Hey!

1187
00:44:52,720 --> 00:44:55,189
- I Can Do It Alone Very Well! - I Can Do It Alone Very Well!

1188
00:44:55,289 --> 00:44:57,330
It's time for the second guest.

1189
00:44:57,789 --> 00:45:01,260
I heard that as soon as this person's teaser came out,

1190
00:45:01,260 --> 00:45:04,800
people got ready to shop for this person's items.

1191
00:45:04,800 --> 00:45:06,069
- Really? - I love those kind of people.

1192
00:45:06,069 --> 00:45:07,300
She has a lot of must-have items.

1193
00:45:07,300 --> 00:45:09,709
- Uhm Jung Hwa and Shin Ha Kyun. - Right.

1194
00:45:09,709 --> 00:45:12,979
She was a rookie actress that showed her great presence...

1195
00:45:12,979 --> 00:45:13,979
amongst these great seniors.

1196
00:45:13,979 --> 00:45:15,379
- Her acting was great. - That's something hard to do.

1197
00:45:15,379 --> 00:45:17,479
Welcome, Jo Aram!

1198
00:45:17,479 --> 00:45:18,709
(Jo Aram)

1199
00:45:19,410 --> 00:45:21,149
(Hello)

1200
00:45:21,149 --> 00:45:22,419
- Hello! - Hello!

1201
00:45:23,689 --> 00:45:25,419
(The rising star that lives a smart life alone)

1202
00:45:25,419 --> 00:45:26,419
(Home Alone)

1203
00:45:26,419 --> 00:45:28,859
(Hey! I Can Do It Alone Very Well)

1204
00:45:29,459 --> 00:45:32,830
(She got everyone's attention with her extraordinary charisma.)

1205
00:45:33,959 --> 00:45:37,569
(A talented rookie that suits any kind of role)

1206
00:45:38,769 --> 00:45:40,140
(Actress Jo Aram)

1207
00:45:41,600 --> 00:45:43,609
- Gosh, hello! - Hello.

1208
00:45:43,609 --> 00:45:45,269
- Welcome. - Gosh!

1209
00:45:45,269 --> 00:45:46,479
- Hello. - Welcome.

1210
00:45:46,479 --> 00:45:48,539
Hello, I'm Actress Jo Aram.

1211
00:45:48,539 --> 00:45:50,379
- It's great to meet you all. - Gosh.

1212
00:45:50,379 --> 00:45:51,749
(It's great to see you.)

1213
00:45:52,050 --> 00:45:54,479
- What year were you born in? - I was born in 2000.

1214
00:45:54,720 --> 00:45:56,149
She's a millennium baby.

1215
00:45:56,220 --> 00:45:57,689
Do you not know anyone here?

1216
00:45:57,789 --> 00:46:00,120
- I don't. - I heard that there was someone...

1217
00:46:00,120 --> 00:46:02,060
who really wanted to meet here.

1218
00:46:02,060 --> 00:46:03,660
- Who is it? - Who is it?

1219
00:46:03,660 --> 00:46:05,129
(Really?)

1220
00:46:05,189 --> 00:46:08,399
I really like Wilson.

1221
00:46:08,459 --> 00:46:09,729
What?

1222
00:46:10,200 --> 00:46:12,830
He was chosen quite a lot as someone...

1223
00:46:12,830 --> 00:46:14,640
- who the guests wanted to meet. - Right.

1224
00:46:14,640 --> 00:46:15,700
He was mentioned often.

1225
00:46:16,269 --> 00:46:21,910
(Kian84 was the celebrity of the new members.)

1226
00:46:21,979 --> 00:46:23,780
You must've been quite excited to get mentioned.

1227
00:46:23,780 --> 00:46:25,209
(It's time to get into Aram's life.)

1228
00:46:25,879 --> 00:46:28,319
(Decorating Daily Life One by One)

1229
00:46:32,149 --> 00:46:33,260
Where is this?

1230
00:46:33,760 --> 00:46:35,019
Are there crickets here?

1231
00:46:36,689 --> 00:46:37,830
That was your alarm?

1232
00:46:37,830 --> 00:46:38,859
- That was your alarm? - Yes.

1233
00:46:39,289 --> 00:46:40,530
- You can get up with the sound? - That's nice.

1234
00:46:40,530 --> 00:46:42,530
Yes. I like the sounds of nature.

1235
00:46:42,530 --> 00:46:43,800
I see.

1236
00:46:45,069 --> 00:46:47,039
(She puts her cuddle toy to the side.)

1237
00:46:48,339 --> 00:46:50,970
(She greets the morning with the sound of crickets.)

1238
00:46:52,939 --> 00:46:54,709
(She moisturizes herself with the face mist by her bed.)

1239
00:46:54,709 --> 00:46:57,350
- Do you have dry skin? - It's really dry.

1240
00:46:59,350 --> 00:47:01,780
(Wriggling)

1241
00:47:03,649 --> 00:47:06,319
(She fixes her sleep socks.)

1242
00:47:06,519 --> 00:47:08,560
- You sleep with sleep socks on. - Yes.

1243
00:47:08,560 --> 00:47:10,189
I've seen a few who wear sleep socks.

1244
00:47:12,530 --> 00:47:13,859
Hello.

1245
00:47:13,859 --> 00:47:16,030
I have lived alone for a year and three months.

1246
00:47:16,030 --> 00:47:17,430
I am Jo Aram.

1247
00:47:17,430 --> 00:47:19,600
(I am Jo Aram.)

1248
00:47:21,769 --> 00:47:23,370
The room is well-lit.

1249
00:47:23,939 --> 00:47:26,269
(She embraces the sun as soon as she gets up.)

1250
00:47:31,780 --> 00:47:33,680
(Flapping)

1251
00:47:34,819 --> 00:47:37,519
(She makes her bed right away.)

1252
00:47:42,789 --> 00:47:47,300
It has been around three months since I have moved to this place.

1253
00:47:47,959 --> 00:47:50,600
(Aram's new house)

1254
00:47:50,970 --> 00:47:52,970
The overall atmosphere of the house...

1255
00:47:52,970 --> 00:47:54,839
was made to be cozy...

1256
00:47:54,839 --> 00:47:57,740
and like a place of rest.

1257
00:47:57,740 --> 00:47:59,609
I tried my best to make it feel comfortable.

1258
00:48:00,839 --> 00:48:02,640
Check out her kitchen. Gosh.

1259
00:48:02,740 --> 00:48:04,109
It's so neat.

1260
00:48:05,780 --> 00:48:07,419
She's really diligent.

1261
00:48:07,419 --> 00:48:08,550
(Aram's taste can be seen here and there.)

1262
00:48:08,749 --> 00:48:10,249
As for the bedroom,

1263
00:48:10,450 --> 00:48:13,459
I'm someone who is serious about getting good sleep.

1264
00:48:13,760 --> 00:48:17,530
The importance of sleep is really big to me.

1265
00:48:17,530 --> 00:48:18,660
It really is important.

1266
00:48:20,330 --> 00:48:22,859
It was based on feng shui.

1267
00:48:22,859 --> 00:48:26,269
That fake wall was set up based on feng shui.

1268
00:48:27,740 --> 00:48:30,470
(Fake wall based on feng shui)

1269
00:48:30,470 --> 00:48:31,939
- What? - Why?

1270
00:48:31,939 --> 00:48:35,209
I heard that it's good not to see the door while sleeping.

1271
00:48:35,209 --> 00:48:36,749
- Really? - Yes.

1272
00:48:36,810 --> 00:48:38,879
Do you order that wall on purpose?

1273
00:48:38,879 --> 00:48:41,180
I brought it from my previous house.

1274
00:48:41,319 --> 00:48:43,019
As it didn't fit perfectly,

1275
00:48:43,019 --> 00:48:44,519
I had to cut it on my own.

1276
00:48:44,519 --> 00:48:47,560
Did things work out after setting up that wall?

1277
00:48:47,560 --> 00:48:49,419
Yes. Well, I get good sleep.

1278
00:48:49,930 --> 00:48:52,530
She's young, but she's interested in these things too.

1279
00:48:53,260 --> 00:48:55,700
(A fake wall that guarantees great sleep)

1280
00:48:56,930 --> 00:49:00,300
(She uses the space of the inside of the wall.)

1281
00:49:01,169 --> 00:49:02,800
(The outside is decorated with the things she likes.)

1282
00:49:02,800 --> 00:49:04,740
That's a postcard of my favorite movie.

1283
00:49:05,339 --> 00:49:07,209
(She decorated the wall...)

1284
00:49:07,209 --> 00:49:08,810
(by hanging up the things she likes on her own.)

1285
00:49:09,709 --> 00:49:11,649
(By the kitchen,)

1286
00:49:12,050 --> 00:49:13,680
(there's a curtain that she hung up on her own!)

1287
00:49:14,319 --> 00:49:16,689
A lot of people separate that area as well.

1288
00:49:17,249 --> 00:49:18,850
(If you open the door besides that area,)

1289
00:49:19,720 --> 00:49:20,859
(the utility room is filled with Aram's touch as well.)

1290
00:49:20,859 --> 00:49:22,819
- You put up those curtains too? - Yes.

1291
00:49:22,990 --> 00:49:24,789
I think it makes a big difference to have things covered.

1292
00:49:24,789 --> 00:49:27,200
- Right. - She likes having things covered.

1293
00:49:27,200 --> 00:49:29,459
I covered all of the cleaning items.

1294
00:49:29,459 --> 00:49:31,800
They must be covered. That's really pretty.

1295
00:49:32,629 --> 00:49:34,399
I like traveling.

1296
00:49:34,600 --> 00:49:38,609
The postcards or items I bought while traveling...

1297
00:49:38,839 --> 00:49:40,879
that reveal my taste...

1298
00:49:40,879 --> 00:49:42,709
- are around the house. - That's so cute.

1299
00:49:44,050 --> 00:49:45,450
(The items are filled with her taste.)

1300
00:49:45,450 --> 00:49:46,780
- That's for tissues? - Yes.

1301
00:49:48,120 --> 00:49:49,649
Gosh, that's so cute.

1302
00:49:49,950 --> 00:49:51,050
She must like rabbits.

1303
00:49:51,050 --> 00:49:55,120
It's a house where I can fully rest.

1304
00:49:58,060 --> 00:50:00,600
I can't believe everything is kept so white and neat.

1305
00:50:04,999 --> 00:50:07,069
(Rolling)

1306
00:50:13,010 --> 00:50:14,539
(Feeling shy)

1307
00:50:14,539 --> 00:50:17,109
Is that really your bare face?

1308
00:50:17,109 --> 00:50:18,810
- Yes. - That's your bare face?

1309
00:50:20,680 --> 00:50:22,120
Didn't you forget to wash up your makeup from last night?

1310
00:50:27,419 --> 00:50:29,390
- She really has great skin. - I'm so jealous.

1311
00:50:29,390 --> 00:50:30,560
That's the skin of a human being? Gosh.

1312
00:50:38,030 --> 00:50:40,439
(She keeps her sleep socks on even in the restroom.)

1313
00:50:40,439 --> 00:50:41,839
You didn't put on the slippers?

1314
00:50:41,839 --> 00:50:43,769
I didn't. Why didn't I put them on?

1315
00:50:44,140 --> 00:50:46,240
- You feel shy, right? - I feel really shy.

1316
00:50:46,240 --> 00:50:48,039
- Right. - I feel so embarrassed.

1317
00:50:48,580 --> 00:50:49,609
This is fun to watch.

1318
00:50:50,810 --> 00:50:52,910
Hold on.

1319
00:50:53,350 --> 00:50:55,080
- What's so fun to watch, Kian84? - What are you saying?

1320
00:50:55,080 --> 00:50:56,819
- It's fun to watch how she lives. - What's so fun to watch?

1321
00:50:56,919 --> 00:50:59,120
It's really fun to watch how someone else lives life.

1322
00:50:59,120 --> 00:51:00,289
We watched different people live their lives every week.

1323
00:51:00,289 --> 00:51:01,819
We watch how others live every week.

1324
00:51:01,819 --> 00:51:03,390
So this is why people watch Home Alone!

1325
00:51:03,390 --> 00:51:04,830
What on earth is he saying?

1326
00:51:07,800 --> 00:51:10,129
(Feeling refreshed)

1327
00:51:12,269 --> 00:51:15,640
(That really woke me up.)

1328
00:51:17,810 --> 00:51:23,039
(She applies the skin lotion before the moisture goes away.)

1329
00:51:25,209 --> 00:51:29,149
(She takes care of her skin as soon as she gets up.)

1330
00:51:30,649 --> 00:51:32,149
So there are people who actually do that.

1331
00:51:32,149 --> 00:51:33,990
I do that every day too.

1332
00:51:33,990 --> 00:51:35,660
- Right. - We have Ki Bum too.

1333
00:51:39,430 --> 00:51:44,300
(She's setting up a wall of moisture unlike anyone else.)

1334
00:51:45,870 --> 00:51:49,100
(She's done with her morning routine.)

1335
00:51:50,439 --> 00:51:53,479
(She rushes somewhere after taking care of her skin.)

1336
00:51:55,410 --> 00:51:56,510
What is that?

1337
00:51:57,580 --> 00:51:59,810
- It's a hygrometer. - I don't have a hygrometer.

1338
00:52:00,149 --> 00:52:01,950
It's to check if they have enough water?

1339
00:52:01,950 --> 00:52:05,519
Yes. It's red if they are dry.

1340
00:52:05,749 --> 00:52:07,819
It's green if they are moist.

1341
00:52:08,319 --> 00:52:09,519
It's blue if they are completely wet.

1342
00:52:09,519 --> 00:52:11,890
I usually give them water when around two leaves are dead.

1343
00:52:13,060 --> 00:52:14,600
That means they are really strong.

1344
00:52:15,030 --> 00:52:16,600
They're trying hard to survive.

1345
00:52:16,600 --> 00:52:18,330
She's actually using a hygrometer.

1346
00:52:18,330 --> 00:52:19,370
That's amazing.

1347
00:52:20,640 --> 00:52:21,970
(She takes the dry plants after checking with the hygrometer.)

1348
00:52:21,970 --> 00:52:24,169
- It's really convenient. - That's nice.

1349
00:52:26,269 --> 00:52:29,910
(She checks the temperature of the water meticulously.)

1350
00:52:29,910 --> 00:52:32,180
I can tell she's a really neat person.

1351
00:52:32,850 --> 00:52:34,249
You changed the shower head, right?

1352
00:52:34,249 --> 00:52:35,479
You're right.

1353
00:52:37,390 --> 00:52:40,490
(It's time to wake the plants up as well.)

1354
00:52:41,419 --> 00:52:43,789
(You guys have to get moisturized too.)

1355
00:52:44,459 --> 00:52:47,660
(All living creatures in the house have been moisturized.)

1356
00:52:50,430 --> 00:52:53,229
(She heads to one side of the living room.)

1357
00:52:55,539 --> 00:52:57,640
- There's a fish tank. - Yes.

1358
00:52:58,839 --> 00:53:02,140
They are my pet fish.

1359
00:53:03,240 --> 00:53:06,379
I have raised them for six years.

1360
00:53:06,609 --> 00:53:08,749
It has been that long since I have raised them.

1361
00:53:08,749 --> 00:53:11,450
Then you've raised them since you were in high school.

1362
00:53:11,450 --> 00:53:12,490
Yes.

1363
00:53:12,950 --> 00:53:14,859
- You were in high school... - Yes.

1364
00:53:14,859 --> 00:53:15,959
six years ago.

1365
00:53:16,689 --> 00:53:18,959
- I was still in my 40s 6 years ago. - Gosh!

1366
00:53:18,959 --> 00:53:20,359
- You're a goblin. - You're right.

1367
00:53:20,359 --> 00:53:21,830
- You're younger than I thought. - I'm Mooblin.

1368
00:53:22,560 --> 00:53:24,229
- Gosh. - The Gong Yoo of Home Alone.

1369
00:53:24,229 --> 00:53:25,499
(The Gong Yoo of Home Alone)

1370
00:53:25,499 --> 00:53:27,300
- The router. - The router.

1371
00:53:28,399 --> 00:53:29,740
I have nine fish.

1372
00:53:29,740 --> 00:53:30,810
Their names are...

1373
00:53:31,839 --> 00:53:33,269
Ketchup, Mustard,

1374
00:53:33,269 --> 00:53:34,280
(Ketchup)

1375
00:53:34,280 --> 00:53:35,979
(Mustard)

1376
00:53:36,479 --> 00:53:38,350
Nacho, Cheese, and Garlic.

1377
00:53:38,410 --> 00:53:40,550
These five have names.

1378
00:53:40,879 --> 00:53:42,479
They look so pretty.

1379
00:53:43,120 --> 00:53:44,450
They aren't seawater fish, right?

1380
00:53:44,450 --> 00:53:46,959
- They aren't. - They are Cichlids.

1381
00:53:46,990 --> 00:53:48,390
You're right!

1382
00:53:48,390 --> 00:53:50,530
They must be raised just amongst themselves.

1383
00:53:50,530 --> 00:53:52,490
- You're right. - They are very violent.

1384
00:53:52,490 --> 00:53:53,700
You're right!

1385
00:53:53,700 --> 00:53:55,129
He's into vivariums.

1386
00:53:55,660 --> 00:53:57,370
- Actually, - She must love cooking too.

1387
00:53:57,370 --> 00:53:58,999
I really enjoyed the episode with your lizards.

1388
00:53:58,999 --> 00:54:00,870
- Gosh! - Right.

1389
00:54:00,870 --> 00:54:02,669
- He loves animals. - Right.

1390
00:54:02,769 --> 00:54:05,069
This is the best episode I've seen in a while too.

1391
00:54:05,709 --> 00:54:07,080
(You're right.)

1392
00:54:07,240 --> 00:54:09,539
How did you end up liking fish?

1393
00:54:10,310 --> 00:54:12,249
Ever since I was young,

1394
00:54:12,379 --> 00:54:14,879
- I enjoyed going to aquariums. - Right.

1395
00:54:14,879 --> 00:54:16,120
As I couldn't go all the time,

1396
00:54:16,120 --> 00:54:19,990
I wanted to make myself my own aquarium in the house.

1397
00:54:19,990 --> 00:54:22,289
When I became a college student,

1398
00:54:22,459 --> 00:54:25,160
I got the most amount of money during Seollal.

1399
00:54:25,160 --> 00:54:26,689
- For making bows? - Right.

1400
00:54:26,689 --> 00:54:28,229
- With that money... - You used that money.

1401
00:54:28,229 --> 00:54:29,899
Yes. I went to the aquarium street in Dongdaemun...

1402
00:54:29,899 --> 00:54:31,470
and set that up.

1403
00:54:31,470 --> 00:54:33,470
You had that in your parent's house and you brought it with you?

1404
00:54:33,470 --> 00:54:35,800
Yes. I moved the whole tank to my house.

1405
00:54:38,370 --> 00:54:40,479
(Rushing)

1406
00:54:42,180 --> 00:54:44,479
(She opens up the curtain...)

1407
00:54:45,580 --> 00:54:48,919
(and grabs something.)

1408
00:54:48,919 --> 00:54:50,050
What is she up to? Cleaning?

1409
00:54:51,319 --> 00:54:53,490
(She heads to the fish tank after getting something.)

1410
00:54:55,620 --> 00:54:57,129
(Putting together)

1411
00:54:59,229 --> 00:55:00,260
What is that?

1412
00:55:00,260 --> 00:55:01,359
It's a cleaning tool.

1413
00:55:01,359 --> 00:55:02,930
- It's to suck in the water. - It's a siphon.

1414
00:55:02,930 --> 00:55:04,129
- A siphon? - Yes.

1415
00:55:04,300 --> 00:55:05,830
- To fill it with new water? - Yes.

1416
00:55:07,700 --> 00:55:10,370
(She puts the cleaning tools to the side.)

1417
00:55:11,740 --> 00:55:14,180
(Let's wash my hands before I clean the fish tank.)

1418
00:55:14,740 --> 00:55:16,010
Is it not done with gloves on?

1419
00:55:16,010 --> 00:55:18,149
Gloves can also be bad for the fish,

1420
00:55:18,149 --> 00:55:19,580
so it's best to use bare hands.

1421
00:55:19,580 --> 00:55:21,780
I wash my hands just with water.

1422
00:55:21,780 --> 00:55:23,149
You have to be careful with chemical substances.

1423
00:55:23,149 --> 00:55:24,390
Right.

1424
00:55:26,689 --> 00:55:28,589
(Wiping)

1425
00:55:30,859 --> 00:55:32,930
(She starts with the wall of the fish tank.)

1426
00:55:32,930 --> 00:55:34,160
Hold on.

1427
00:55:34,160 --> 00:55:35,560
(Wiping)

1428
00:55:36,499 --> 00:55:38,030
Gosh, it looks really clean.

1429
00:55:38,870 --> 00:55:41,970
I'm wiping off things like moss.

1430
00:55:43,300 --> 00:55:46,370
As it's by the window, it slightly becomes mossy.

1431
00:55:46,370 --> 00:55:49,379
The moss isn't bad for the fish,

1432
00:55:49,580 --> 00:55:52,580
but it can look dirty from the outside.

1433
00:55:52,580 --> 00:55:55,280
That is why I wipe it away with the sponge first.

1434
00:55:55,280 --> 00:55:57,249
(Getting rid of the moss with the sponge)

1435
00:55:58,120 --> 00:55:59,789
(Heave-ho)

1436
00:56:00,459 --> 00:56:02,589
(She bites onto her lips.)

1437
00:56:03,760 --> 00:56:05,189
That is a lot of work.

1438
00:56:07,800 --> 00:56:09,459
(Moving)

1439
00:56:10,200 --> 00:56:12,669
(She takes the next cleaning tool after getting rid of the moss.)

1440
00:56:16,269 --> 00:56:18,069
(Pressing)

1441
00:56:20,510 --> 00:56:21,609
- What is that? - The siphon.

1442
00:56:21,609 --> 00:56:24,080
(What is she doing?)

1443
00:56:30,700 --> 00:56:33,430
I use a siphon used for cleaning.

1444
00:56:35,000 --> 00:56:36,569
It's to suck up the waste on the ground.

1445
00:56:36,569 --> 00:56:38,569
The light waste gets sucked up with the water.

1446
00:56:39,439 --> 00:56:40,609
- What about the sand? - So that's cleaned too.

1447
00:56:40,609 --> 00:56:41,980
The sand goes back down.

1448
00:56:41,980 --> 00:56:43,680
- It goes back down as it's heavy. - That's fascinating.

1449
00:56:43,680 --> 00:56:44,879
There's a tool that does that?

1450
00:56:45,109 --> 00:56:46,710
(Fascinated)

1451
00:56:47,750 --> 00:56:49,319
It's referred to as the water breaks.

1452
00:56:49,680 --> 00:56:52,719
It literally means the water becomes dirty.

1453
00:56:52,719 --> 00:56:54,219
It becomes murky.

1454
00:56:54,219 --> 00:56:57,060
To avoid the water from breaking,

1455
00:56:57,060 --> 00:56:59,189
the tank has to be cleaned to a certain extent.

1456
00:56:59,290 --> 00:57:00,359
It can't be cleaned too much.

1457
00:57:00,359 --> 00:57:03,899
The bacteria must be living for the fish to live.

1458
00:57:03,960 --> 00:57:05,770
So that is how I clean the fish tank.

1459
00:57:07,770 --> 00:57:09,500
The fish will die if it's too clean.

1460
00:57:09,600 --> 00:57:10,799
Right.

1461
00:57:11,239 --> 00:57:13,040
- There has to be some poop. - Right.

1462
00:57:13,040 --> 00:57:14,779
(There has to be some poop.)

1463
00:57:14,779 --> 00:57:16,609
That is a fascinating world.

1464
00:57:17,109 --> 00:57:18,250
You poopers!

1465
00:57:21,850 --> 00:57:23,450
Gosh, what is that?

1466
00:57:23,450 --> 00:57:24,649
- That's so much poop! - They're so nice.

1467
00:57:24,649 --> 00:57:26,020
They only pooped in one place.

1468
00:57:26,620 --> 00:57:28,790
- They're all potty-trained. - Potty-trained?

1469
00:57:28,790 --> 00:57:30,660
- How did you make that happen? - How did you make that happen?

1470
00:57:30,859 --> 00:57:35,299
Those fish dig up the sand with their mouths...

1471
00:57:35,299 --> 00:57:38,669
or live inside those rocks.

1472
00:57:39,029 --> 00:57:42,040
The waste gets collected into the rocks that don't get chosen.

1473
00:57:42,040 --> 00:57:43,140
They use them as toilets.

1474
00:57:43,140 --> 00:57:44,569
- Right. - They're so smart.

1475
00:57:44,569 --> 00:57:46,339
They are certain about their own areas.

1476
00:57:46,540 --> 00:57:48,480
Right. They are known for that.

1477
00:57:49,180 --> 00:57:51,149
- They are so smart. - That's nice of them.

1478
00:57:52,210 --> 00:57:54,180
(Feeling proud)

1479
00:57:57,549 --> 00:57:59,790
(She is completely focused on cleaning the fish tank.)

1480
00:58:00,890 --> 00:58:02,160
(She carried on for a while.)

1481
00:58:02,160 --> 00:58:04,529
That's so much work. When will she get done?

1482
00:58:05,460 --> 00:58:06,660
But you enjoy doing that, right?

1483
00:58:06,660 --> 00:58:08,700
Yes. It clears me from my thoughts.

1484
00:58:09,100 --> 00:58:10,529
She has a great sense of responsibility.

1485
00:58:10,529 --> 00:58:12,569
(This should be enough!)

1486
00:58:14,000 --> 00:58:17,370
(She discards the fish waste she collected diligently.)

1487
00:58:19,410 --> 00:58:20,509
(Grabbing)

1488
00:58:21,480 --> 00:58:22,640
(Touching)

1489
00:58:23,810 --> 00:58:25,509
- What are you doing? - The temperature...

1490
00:58:25,509 --> 00:58:28,020
- Do you do it with your hand? - Yes, I check it with my hand.

1491
00:58:28,980 --> 00:58:30,379
I do it by hunch.

1492
00:58:30,379 --> 00:58:31,489
(After checking the temperature, she comes back with water.)

1493
00:58:32,890 --> 00:58:35,259
(Touching the water here and there)

1494
00:58:38,060 --> 00:58:40,089
(She checks the temperature of both tanks with her hands.)

1495
00:58:41,359 --> 00:58:42,430
What's that?

1496
00:58:43,060 --> 00:58:44,969
I need to do a water change.

1497
00:58:45,799 --> 00:58:47,799
The temperature needs to be similar.

1498
00:58:47,799 --> 00:58:50,500
I've cleaned the fish tanks for many years.

1499
00:58:50,640 --> 00:58:52,109
Back in the day, I needed a thermometer.

1500
00:58:52,109 --> 00:58:55,480
Now, I can measure the temperature by using my hands.

1501
00:58:55,609 --> 00:58:56,910
So I match the temperature that way.

1502
00:58:56,910 --> 00:58:58,779
(Her golden hands let her measure the temperature.)

1503
00:59:00,210 --> 00:59:02,719
I add the water with a gentle flow so that the fish don't get startled.

1504
00:59:02,719 --> 00:59:04,549
- That's how I clean the tanks. - What's that water?

1505
00:59:04,689 --> 00:59:07,750
That's tap water I collected a day before.

1506
00:59:07,750 --> 00:59:10,319
- You need to do it in advance. - It's water with chlorine remover.

1507
00:59:10,319 --> 00:59:11,390
That's right.

1508
00:59:12,960 --> 00:59:17,730
(I'm proud of my work.)

1509
00:59:18,899 --> 00:59:20,930
(After cleaning the fish tanks)

1510
00:59:21,640 --> 00:59:23,669
(Opening)

1511
00:59:24,399 --> 00:59:27,169
(What should I eat?)

1512
00:59:28,710 --> 00:59:30,379
You cook.

1513
00:59:31,210 --> 00:59:32,350
It's clean.

1514
00:59:32,750 --> 00:59:34,180
- I enjoy cooking. - There are many things.

1515
00:59:34,180 --> 00:59:36,149
- I know. You divided things up. - She divided things up.

1516
00:59:36,149 --> 00:59:38,589
- Yes. - She's skilled.

1517
00:59:39,250 --> 00:59:40,549
(Grabbing)

1518
00:59:42,989 --> 00:59:46,330
(She takes out side dishes from the fridge.)

1519
00:59:48,299 --> 00:59:52,370
(The frozen rice and soup go into the microwave.)

1520
00:59:54,640 --> 00:59:56,439
Are those side dishes from your parents?

1521
00:59:56,439 --> 00:59:58,210
I made them myself.

1522
00:59:58,210 --> 01:00:01,109
- Did you make those side dishes? - Yes, I make them myself.

1523
01:00:01,279 --> 01:00:03,009
I've seen many people make...

1524
01:00:03,009 --> 01:00:04,109
- their own main dishes, - Seriously.

1525
01:00:04,109 --> 01:00:05,149
but not side dishes.

1526
01:00:05,149 --> 01:00:07,779
Most people eat their main dishes with their moms' side dishes.

1527
01:00:07,879 --> 01:00:09,080
She's very skilled.

1528
01:00:09,879 --> 01:00:10,980
Seriously.

1529
01:00:12,319 --> 01:00:15,819
Since I live alone, ordering food always yields leftovers.

1530
01:00:17,319 --> 01:00:18,689
That's why I don't order food.

1531
01:00:19,129 --> 01:00:20,589
You feel guilty if you get a lot of leftovers.

1532
01:00:20,589 --> 01:00:21,600
That's right.

1533
01:00:21,600 --> 01:00:23,160
(She transfers the beef and radish soup.)

1534
01:00:23,160 --> 01:00:25,330
- You divide it into small portions. - Yes.

1535
01:00:25,330 --> 01:00:27,399
I make the soup,

1536
01:00:27,569 --> 01:00:29,169
divide it into small portions, and freeze it.

1537
01:00:30,540 --> 01:00:33,040
